1
00:00:06,599 --> 00:00:10,280
A major river in the heart
of Europe, which at this

2
00:00:10,292 --> 00:00:14,199
location presents a natural,
almost untamed appearance.

3
00:00:14,199 --> 00:00:18,150
It's hard to believe that the flow
dynamics are the result of human

4
00:00:18,162 --> 00:00:22,300
intervention, achieved through
strategic modifications to the riverbed.

5
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,988
They create a diversity
of currents that visibly

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
invigorates the water
and its environment.

7
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:36,700
And time and again, for a few seconds, you
can see the water spiraling into vortices.

8
00:00:40,899 --> 00:00:45,899
Such vortices can be permanently
generated in the laboratory.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,100
A tempest in a teacup that evokes
the natural power of tornadoes.

10
00:00:51,299 --> 00:00:54,899
Is it possible to generate
energy using vortices?

11
00:00:54,899 --> 00:00:57,899
Or perhaps use them to conserve energy?

12
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:04,318
In the process of enriching water
with oxygen, a hyperbolic vortex

13
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:09,099
funnel proves to be more efficient
than traditional aeration methods.

14
00:01:09,099 --> 00:01:13,799
This has been demonstrated through
scientific laboratory experiments.

15
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,377
In practical applications,
numerous vortex funnels are already

16
00:01:18,389 --> 00:01:22,500
being used to oxygenate ponds
and even clarification ponds.

17
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:32,236
In the cooling systems of large industrial
facilities, hyperbolic vortex generators

18
00:01:32,248 --> 00:01:37,400
are now employed, eliminating the
need for chemicals in the cooling water.

19
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:47,400
Fascinating vortex phenomena also
develop in egg-shaped containers.

20
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:51,357
These containers have
been used for many years

21
00:01:51,369 --> 00:01:54,200
in the production of
medical preparations.

22
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:01,200
All these incredible visuals would
not have been possible without them.

23
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:05,352
Viktor Schauberger and his son
Walter Schauberger, through their

24
00:02:05,364 --> 00:02:09,400
inventions and patents, also
laid the foundation for this film.

25
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:17,522
Some members of the Schauberger
family lived and conducted

26
00:02:17,534 --> 00:02:21,500
research at the PKS Villa
near Bad Ischl in Upper Austria.

27
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:25,864
Viktor Schauberger,
shown here in a photo

28
00:02:25,876 --> 00:02:29,099
from his younger years,
passed away in 1958.

29
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:34,300
His son, Walter
Schauberger, lived until 1994.

30
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:38,889
Their visionary ideas
and concepts, along with

31
00:02:38,901 --> 00:02:42,199
their warnings, are more
relevant today than ever.

32
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:01,834
It's springtime.

33
00:03:02,599 --> 00:03:06,900
The Clever Spring near Wildalpen
in Styria comes to life anew.

34
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:14,199
When the snow melts on the Hochschwab
massif, the mountain releases its water.

35
00:03:14,199 --> 00:03:17,838
Jörg Schauberger and
Ottmar Grober marvel at this

36
00:03:17,850 --> 00:03:21,500
natural spectacle once
again, and for good reason.

37
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:26,518
Few images capture the
essence of pristine nature as

38
00:03:26,530 --> 00:03:30,400
effectively as the sight of a
bubbling mountain stream.

39
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:40,300
As the water swirls around
each stone, it pulls air into itself.

40
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:42,000
The water breathes.

41
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:50,157
Jörg's grandfather, Viktor
Schauberger, also envisioned

42
00:03:50,169 --> 00:03:53,599
such a revitalization
of water in our rivers.

43
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:59,888
From his book, which contains numerous
original texts by Viktor Schauberger,

44
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:04,599
Jörg quotes his grandfather on
the desired revitalization of water.

45
00:04:04,599 --> 00:04:08,729
Today's watercourse management
does not take into consideration

46
00:04:08,741 --> 00:04:12,300
the crucial aspect of the
water's inner revitalization.

47
00:04:15,099 --> 00:04:19,800
The book features numerous essays by
Viktor Schauberger on river management.

48
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:27,497
One of his conclusions was that you
don't manage a river or stream from

49
00:04:27,509 --> 00:04:32,500
the banks; instead, you implement
structures within the river itself.

50
00:04:56,451 --> 00:05:01,016
From the riverbank, it's difficult
to discern what river engineer

51
00:05:01,041 --> 00:05:05,964
Ottmar Grober has integrated into
the Mur, the longest river in Styria.

52
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:09,624
In 2005, he placed
massive boulders to form a

53
00:05:09,636 --> 00:05:12,899
roughly 50-meter-long
funnel in the riverbed.

54
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:17,300
Two more large sediment funnels were added.

55
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:21,367
The purpose of these installations
is to enhance the diversity

56
00:05:21,379 --> 00:05:25,199
of water flow and to better
structure the riverbed profile.

57
00:05:25,199 --> 00:05:29,885
Before-and-after measurements
show significant changes in the riverbed.

58
00:05:29,897 --> 00:05:34,399
As a result, improved living
conditions for native fish were created.

59
00:05:35,899 --> 00:05:39,072
During monitoring,
three fish species typical

60
00:05:39,084 --> 00:05:42,199
of rivers were only
found in the funnel area.

61
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,038
A positive aspect is
that the banks in this

62
00:05:51,050 --> 00:05:54,100
section are lined with
densely packed trees.

63
00:05:54,899 --> 00:05:58,205
These trees provide the
river shading that Viktor

64
00:05:58,217 --> 00:06:01,800
Schauberger advocated for,
which helps cool the water.

65
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,926
Even in aerial photographs,
only the surface

66
00:06:09,938 --> 00:06:13,399
currents caused by the
Mur installations are visible.

67
00:06:18,899 --> 00:06:22,396
From space, however,
satellite cameras can clearly

68
00:06:22,408 --> 00:06:26,399
identify the integrated funnel
in the riverbed of the Mur.

69
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:36,877
Approximately 80 kilometers
away in Styria, Ottmar Grober

70
00:06:36,889 --> 00:06:40,899
is on the Schauberger trail
near Neuberg an der Mürz.

71
00:06:40,899 --> 00:06:44,468
It was here that Viktor
Schauberger constructed one

72
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:48,199
of his renowned timber
flotation systems in the 1920s.

73
00:06:48,199 --> 00:06:52,100
Excerpts from a 1929 documentary film.

74
00:07:04,330 --> 00:07:07,630
Today, a photo exhibition
in one of the tunnels

75
00:07:07,630 --> 00:07:11,235
serves as a reminder of
the facility's construction.

76
00:07:15,699 --> 00:07:19,899
Ottmar Grober has thoroughly
examined the construction details.

77
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:26,838
By incorporating specific structures into the
floating channel, Schauberger generated water

78
00:07:26,850 --> 00:07:33,100
vortices, enabling him to better manage the
outgoing round logs, also referred to as blocks.

79
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:39,069
Viktor Schauberger successfully used his
installations to direct the flow through the center of

80
00:07:39,081 --> 00:07:45,000
the floating channel, allowing the blocks to move
along the channel without striking the banks.

81
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:58,566
And that's precisely what I was seeking in my river engineering
endeavors: to channel the water through the center without it

82
00:07:58,578 --> 00:08:07,000
striking the banks, thereby preventing significant damage and
potentially eliminating the need for the bank protections we use today.

83
00:08:07,311 --> 00:08:11,957
And for that, I am very
grateful to Viktor Schauberger.

84
00:08:14,456 --> 00:08:17,058
Ottmar Grober has
developed several river

85
00:08:17,070 --> 00:08:20,057
engineering techniques,
including micro groynes.

86
00:08:21,757 --> 00:08:27,498
Similar to Kapfenberg, these elements
initially create diverse flow patterns in the

87
00:08:27,510 --> 00:08:33,057
Mürz River and revitalize the water in
line with Viktor Schauberger's principles.

88
00:08:36,456 --> 00:08:39,756
Here, you can observe
entire vortex streets.

89
00:08:42,557 --> 00:08:45,675
Not far from the old
log-driving channel, the

90
00:08:45,687 --> 00:08:49,157
micro groynes also serve
to protect the riverbanks.

91
00:08:49,356 --> 00:08:54,833
Similar to Schauberger's installations, the
micro groynes redirect the current towards

92
00:08:54,845 --> 00:08:59,956
the center of the river, thereby safeguarding
the outer bank from erosive forces.

93
00:09:00,956 --> 00:09:04,697
Another construction method
developed by Ottmar Grober is

94
00:09:04,709 --> 00:09:08,657
the meandering ramp, which
is used to bridge a flow gradient.

95
00:09:09,356 --> 00:09:14,256
The meandering ramp exhibits a unique
feature during periods of high water.

96
00:09:15,657 --> 00:09:24,134
Through these ribs, which are constructed across the riverbed
and interlocked, a leftward-flowing braid, known as a water

97
00:09:24,146 --> 00:09:32,356
braid, forms during high water events. This concentrates the
energy in the center, thereby protecting the outer banks.

98
00:09:37,791 --> 00:09:41,357
Meandering ramps serve
as excellent substitutes for

99
00:09:41,369 --> 00:09:45,290
outdated weirs, as demonstrated
here in the Stübmingbach.

100
00:09:45,290 --> 00:09:50,532
Simultaneously, meandering ramps
serve as eco-friendly fish ladders and are

101
00:09:50,544 --> 00:09:55,590
significantly more cost-effective
than traditional concrete fish ladders.

102
00:09:55,591 --> 00:09:58,812
Meandering ramps comply
with the European Water

103
00:09:58,824 --> 00:10:01,990
Framework Directive's
mandate for connectivity.

104
00:10:03,606 --> 00:10:05,404
Back to the Mur River.

105
00:10:05,656 --> 00:10:08,257
Not far from the first
funnel, Ottmar Grober

106
00:10:08,269 --> 00:10:11,056
has even installed a
double funnel in the river.

107
00:10:11,774 --> 00:10:18,833
The combination of inlet and outlet funnels enhances
living conditions for fish and simultaneously

108
00:10:18,845 --> 00:10:26,274
ensures bank protection, which remains fully effective
even in the river bend visible in the background.

109
00:10:32,075 --> 00:10:36,188
Occasionally, driftwood gets
caught in one of the funnels,

110
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:39,975
and this driftwood is clearly
visible even from space.

111
00:10:45,899 --> 00:10:50,616
Ottmar Grober's construction
techniques are now recognized in the

112
00:10:50,628 --> 00:10:55,500
professional community as Instream
River Training, or IRT for short.

113
00:10:55,725 --> 00:10:59,222
IRT installations can
now be found far beyond

114
00:10:59,234 --> 00:11:03,124
Styria, such as in the
saxonian Lausitz in Germany.

115
00:11:03,299 --> 00:11:07,542
In Horka, Gerd Stellmacher
constructed a meandering ramp in

116
00:11:07,554 --> 00:11:11,597
the Mühlenbach, marking
the first of its kind in Germany.

117
00:11:11,850 --> 00:11:15,315
During winter, a distinctive
feature of Instream

118
00:11:15,327 --> 00:11:18,449
River Training becomes
particularly evident.

119
00:11:22,075 --> 00:11:26,975
The installations are often not visible,
but their effects are unmistakable.

120
00:11:27,674 --> 00:11:31,063
In the frozen stream, a
meandering watercourse

121
00:11:31,075 --> 00:11:34,475
emerges within a narrow
and straight streambed.

122
00:11:46,536 --> 00:11:50,234
The IRT aerial tour
continues to Lower Saxony.

123
00:11:50,375 --> 00:11:54,042
From the Lüneberger Heide,
the Äste and Sewe rivers flow

124
00:11:54,054 --> 00:11:57,475
towards the Elbe, often
within a canal-like riverbed.

125
00:12:02,475 --> 00:12:07,875
Ludwig Tent has introduced numerous
micro groynes to rejuvenate the waterways.

126
00:12:16,174 --> 00:12:20,375
They are composed of locally
typical gravel and stones.

127
00:12:20,475 --> 00:12:25,674
On the constructed groynes, for example,
sea trout have found a spawning ground.

128
00:12:30,274 --> 00:12:35,174
The IRT hopping progresses further
into northern Baden-Württemberg.

129
00:12:38,875 --> 00:12:41,950
The town of Kupferzell
is crossed by the

130
00:12:41,962 --> 00:12:45,274
Kupfer River, which
required revitalization.

131
00:12:45,674 --> 00:12:49,975
The town council and the
mayor opted for the IRT method.

132
00:12:53,174 --> 00:12:56,833
This measure has the advantage
of not requiring any additional

133
00:12:56,845 --> 00:12:59,875
land; I operate solely
within the water body itself.

134
00:13:00,274 --> 00:13:04,174
And that's the other benefit for
the municipality, as in Kupferzell.

135
00:13:04,174 --> 00:13:10,327
We don't need to purchase land on either side of
the water or acquire property specifically for

136
00:13:10,339 --> 00:13:16,375
nearby construction areas. Instead, with this
measure, I can focus directly on the water body.

137
00:13:17,475 --> 00:13:22,975
This IRT initiative was overseen
by civil engineer Erich Linsin.

138
00:13:23,475 --> 00:13:27,774
A walking excavator advanced
meter by meter along the riverbed.

139
00:13:28,075 --> 00:13:29,774
A symbolic image.

140
00:13:29,774 --> 00:13:34,096
The excavator removes stones
from the bank reinforcement.

141
00:13:35,575 --> 00:13:40,375
These boulders are no longer used for
bank protection as stone arrangements.

142
00:13:42,774 --> 00:13:46,435
In Instream River Training,
the stones are integrated

143
00:13:46,447 --> 00:13:49,575
not into the riverbank,
but into the riverbed.

144
00:13:51,075 --> 00:13:56,875
It is crucial that the structures remain
submerged even during low water levels.

145
00:13:58,674 --> 00:14:04,599
Following the completion of the initial
construction phase in 2018, approximately

146
00:14:04,611 --> 00:14:10,475
80 water specialists from Baden-Württemberg
visited the revitalized Kupfer River.

147
00:14:10,475 --> 00:14:13,997
They examined the many
installed vortex funnels and

148
00:14:14,009 --> 00:14:17,475
micro groynes along a
stretch of about 1 kilometer.

149
00:14:17,475 --> 00:14:20,761
Some of these structures
are made of wood,

150
00:14:20,773 --> 00:14:24,224
such as pile constructions
using spruce logs.

151
00:14:31,475 --> 00:14:35,713
Within the still straight riverbed,
the IRT installations have

152
00:14:35,725 --> 00:14:39,975
revitalized the Kupfer River
and enhanced its ecological value.

153
00:14:45,674 --> 00:14:50,674
Next stop on the IRT hopping tour:
Lörrach in the southern Black Forest.

154
00:14:54,174 --> 00:15:01,475
In 2007, Erich Linzin carried out the first
significant IRT initiative in Germany.

155
00:15:03,975 --> 00:15:07,542
In the urban area of
Lörrach, he replaced old sole

156
00:15:07,554 --> 00:15:11,274
thresholds in the Wiese
river with funnels and bolts.

157
00:15:11,274 --> 00:15:14,176
These installations
were part of a project by

158
00:15:14,188 --> 00:15:16,975
the German Federal
Environmental Foundation.

159
00:15:17,375 --> 00:15:21,139
From Lörrach, it's a short
distance to Switzerland,

160
00:15:21,151 --> 00:15:24,274
where many IRT
applications are now in use.

161
00:15:24,975 --> 00:15:29,275
South of Bern, three IRT projects
were successfully completed.

162
00:15:37,975 --> 00:15:42,172
In Flamatt, located in the
canton of Fribourg, a complete

163
00:15:42,184 --> 00:15:46,176
pilot route for IRT construction
methods was developed.

164
00:15:51,375 --> 00:15:56,234
Hydraulic engineer Nils Werdenberg
installed approximately 40 micro

165
00:15:56,246 --> 00:16:01,475
groynes for bank protection and
channel structuring in the lower Taverna.

166
00:16:02,875 --> 00:16:06,300
After the installation, we
conducted a monitoring process

167
00:16:06,312 --> 00:16:09,275
to assess the capabilities
of these installations.

168
00:16:09,275 --> 00:16:12,619
The monitoring lasted for 8
years, and we used various

169
00:16:12,631 --> 00:16:16,475
techniques to investigate these
micro groynes in greater depth.

170
00:16:18,176 --> 00:16:20,875
During this monitoring,
we also made a film.

171
00:16:22,076 --> 00:16:25,775
Coincidentally, we were
outside just as the flood began.

172
00:16:27,975 --> 00:16:35,713
Here, you can observe how the floodwaters press against the riverbank
but are redirected by the micro groynes. This causes the flow

173
00:16:35,725 --> 00:16:43,475
force to shift from the bank into the riverbed, moving towards the
center of the river, preventing any further erosion of the banks.

174
00:16:45,775 --> 00:16:48,880
Additionally, it's
evident that the riverside

175
00:16:48,892 --> 00:16:52,076
vegetation has thrived
over the past few years.

176
00:16:54,875 --> 00:17:01,128
The monitoring also revealed that the micro groynes
are significantly more cost-effective, as we

177
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:07,276
use far fewer boulders in the water compared to
constructing a wall on the riverbank with them.

178
00:17:09,476 --> 00:17:12,175
Werdenberg created this photomontage.

179
00:17:12,875 --> 00:17:17,476
What might Viktor Schauberger have
to say about his river constructions?

180
00:17:18,276 --> 00:17:21,675
Just 10 kilometers
away lies the Scherlibach.

181
00:17:21,675 --> 00:17:29,133
Here, on the initiative of the Watercourse Engineering Interest
Group and commissioned by the municipality of Kölnitz, the

182
00:17:29,145 --> 00:17:36,675
first meandering ramp in Switzerland was built - so far the
steepest meandering ramp - and that in extremely confined space.

183
00:17:36,875 --> 00:17:39,824
Ottmar Grober and
the hydraulic engineer

184
00:17:39,836 --> 00:17:43,375
Matthias Mende were
involved in the construction.

185
00:17:43,776 --> 00:17:48,476
He meets his colleague Nils
Werdenberg by the Aare River near Bern.

186
00:17:50,976 --> 00:17:53,786
Here, following Mende's
proposal, three large

187
00:17:53,798 --> 00:17:56,375
micro groynes were
installed in the river.

188
00:17:59,776 --> 00:18:03,792
A practical challenge for
Mende, as he had just completed

189
00:18:03,804 --> 00:18:07,276
his doctoral thesis on
the topic of micro groynes.

190
00:18:09,976 --> 00:18:12,504
This outer bank of the
Aare needed renovation

191
00:18:12,516 --> 00:18:15,276
about five years ago
due to its dilapidated state.

192
00:18:17,175 --> 00:18:19,969
To simultaneously achieve
ecological enhancement,

193
00:18:19,981 --> 00:18:22,675
a system of three micro
groynes was constructed.

194
00:18:23,675 --> 00:18:26,579
They are about 30 to 35
meters long. They shift the

195
00:18:26,591 --> 00:18:29,675
current away from the outer
bank toward the inner bank.

196
00:18:31,175 --> 00:18:37,076
This means the outer bank is relieved. At the same
time, we achieve a significant increase in flow and

197
00:18:37,088 --> 00:18:43,576
structural diversity, and in this case, above all, a
heightened dynamic at the inner bank, which is desired here.

198
00:18:43,976 --> 00:18:48,188
As demonstrated, micro groynes can
be implemented not only in small and

199
00:18:48,200 --> 00:18:52,776
medium-sized water bodies but also in
large ones exceeding 50 meters in width.

200
00:18:52,776 --> 00:18:56,776
I think, realistically, there
are hardly any limits upwards.

201
00:19:01,276 --> 00:19:07,276
We're heading upwards again now, to mark
another spot in Switzerland on the IRT map.

202
00:19:13,875 --> 00:19:18,976
Currently, there is a multimedia e-book
handbook on Instream River Training.

203
00:19:19,375 --> 00:19:23,776
It includes embedded videos
that readers can play as they wish.

204
00:19:30,776 --> 00:19:34,375
Of course, you can also watch
the video in full-screen mode.

205
00:19:39,223 --> 00:19:44,194
In the chapter on the history of IRT,
there's another excerpt from the old

206
00:19:44,206 --> 00:19:48,923
film about Schauberger's floating
system, featuring the original audio.

207
00:19:54,192 --> 00:20:00,485
Following the historical section, the book
details all the construction methods of Instream

208
00:20:00,497 --> 00:20:06,391
River Training, including their unique
variations, such as cross and S-bar formations.

209
00:20:16,375 --> 00:20:20,158
In the section on the meandering
ramp, Ottmar Grober's ramp

210
00:20:20,170 --> 00:20:23,776
in the Stüpmingbach is
depicted with moderate water flow.

211
00:20:26,476 --> 00:20:29,499
At the same time, the
back-and-forth swinging

212
00:20:29,511 --> 00:20:32,875
water can be observed both
downstream and upstream.

213
00:20:38,675 --> 00:20:42,875
At the end of the book, there
are excerpts from doctoral theses.

214
00:20:43,576 --> 00:20:47,497
In these, Christine Sindelar
and Matthias Mende introduced

215
00:20:47,509 --> 00:20:51,576
the term 'Instream River Training'
to the academic community.

216
00:20:53,875 --> 00:20:57,667
Mende wrote his dissertation
on micro groynes, Sindelar

217
00:20:57,679 --> 00:21:01,076
on the meandering ramp
developed by Ottmar Grober.

218
00:21:04,675 --> 00:21:09,276
Christine Sindelar studied at
the Technical University of Graz.

219
00:21:09,276 --> 00:21:13,282
In the hydraulic engineering
lab there, a 1:10 scale model

220
00:21:13,294 --> 00:21:17,175
of a meandering ramp was
constructed in the flow channel.

221
00:21:17,476 --> 00:21:21,843
After numerous experiments, such
as the simulation of flood waves,

222
00:21:21,855 --> 00:21:25,776
Sindelar calculated design
guidelines for a meandering ramp.

223
00:21:29,476 --> 00:21:34,097
The foundational experiments
conducted in the TU Graz laboratory were

224
00:21:34,109 --> 00:21:38,675
not merely academic exercises;
they were quickly applied in practice.

225
00:21:38,675 --> 00:21:43,518
In 2011, in Leitzberg, Lower
Austria, a meandering ramp was

226
00:21:43,530 --> 00:21:49,276
constructed to replace an old weir
that had been destroyed by flooding.

227
00:21:59,976 --> 00:22:04,175
The installation adhered to
Christine Sindelar's ramp design.

228
00:22:04,175 --> 00:22:06,776
Pictured here during a site inspection.

229
00:22:12,476 --> 00:22:15,777
The completed ramp stretches
over 100 meters, making

230
00:22:15,789 --> 00:22:18,976
it the longest meandering
ramp constructed to date.

231
00:22:24,375 --> 00:22:27,749
A fish survey confirmed
that the Leitzberg ramp

232
00:22:27,761 --> 00:22:31,076
is completely navigable
for local fish species.

233
00:22:32,776 --> 00:22:38,473
The ramp also maintains a dynamic
flow in a low-water channel during

234
00:22:38,485 --> 00:22:44,276
periods of low water, which is
crucial for fish amidst climate change.

235
00:22:48,223 --> 00:22:53,561
Ramp designer Christine Sindelar is
now the head of the hydraulic engineering

236
00:22:53,573 --> 00:22:58,923
laboratory at the University of Natural
Resources and Life Sciences in Vienna.

237
00:22:59,523 --> 00:23:03,423
And what is water master
Ottmar Grober actually doing?

238
00:23:03,423 --> 00:23:09,324
Even after retiring, he remains active as a
consultant for river engineering projects.

239
00:23:15,124 --> 00:23:19,559
Not far away from the meandering
ramp, he pays a visit to Prince

240
00:23:19,571 --> 00:23:24,223
Gunda Kar von und zu Liechtenstein
in Neulenkbach, in Lower Austria.

241
00:23:24,324 --> 00:23:28,243
The landowner has allocated
12 hectares of his property

242
00:23:28,255 --> 00:23:31,624
for the restoration of a
floodplain forest area.

243
00:23:31,624 --> 00:23:36,861
This floodplain forest is situated along
the Labembach, which transforms into

244
00:23:36,873 --> 00:23:42,324
the large Tullen a few kilometers downstream,
eventually merging with the Danube.

245
00:23:42,324 --> 00:23:44,324
So it's about forest and water.

246
00:23:44,423 --> 00:23:51,352
The water quality needs enhancement, which in turn
improves the floodplain forest and the groundwater

247
00:23:51,364 --> 00:23:57,624
situation, allowing us to observe improvements
throughout the entire floodplain forest area.

248
00:23:57,624 --> 00:24:01,723
And I wanted to prove that
by implementing this project.

249
00:24:01,723 --> 00:24:06,723
Lichtenstein's mother personally knew
Walter, the son of Viktor Schauberger.

250
00:24:08,324 --> 00:24:12,124
I have been engaged with Schauberger
for as long as I can remember.

251
00:24:12,423 --> 00:24:16,324
That's why he called his project
the Viktor Schauberger Park.

252
00:24:17,223 --> 00:24:27,891
It was essentially Schauberger's theory and insights that I wanted to
bring to light, and thus they needed to be realized in practice. These

253
00:24:27,903 --> 00:24:38,886
theories and insights from Schauberger appeared so compelling to me that
I thought it would be a pity if they didn't receive broader recognition.

254
00:24:40,023 --> 00:24:42,994
Lichtenstein points to
a prime example that

255
00:24:43,006 --> 00:24:46,124
has been realized here
in the riparian forest.

256
00:24:46,324 --> 00:24:52,112
A significant aspect of Schauberger's
theory and insights is that a body of water

257
00:24:52,124 --> 00:24:57,923
needs to be shaded, as the temperature
factor is critical, even during the summer.

258
00:25:00,423 --> 00:25:05,523
Well, a heated body of water is always
considered poor in terms of quality.

259
00:25:07,223 --> 00:25:11,923
The riparian forest is situated in
the southern part of Neulenkbach.

260
00:25:12,624 --> 00:25:16,272
The project area starts
right here, at this bridge.

261
00:25:18,223 --> 00:25:22,005
Otmar Grober and Gundark
Lichtenstein are examining

262
00:25:22,017 --> 00:25:25,374
the structure known
as the diversion facility.

263
00:25:26,624 --> 00:25:31,051
During periods of low water, most
of the Labenbach's flow is diverted

264
00:25:31,063 --> 00:25:35,248
here and channeled through a
side stream into the riparian forest.

265
00:25:35,248 --> 00:25:37,248
The floodplain has more to offer.

266
00:25:38,147 --> 00:25:40,626
For the water, more than we think.

267
00:25:41,527 --> 00:25:48,510
We humans are drawn into the floodplain, into the
untouched floodplain, just like the water itself,

268
00:25:48,522 --> 00:25:55,027
seeking restoration, because the stream has
actually been returned to its original landscape.

269
00:25:55,426 --> 00:26:00,594
The diversion of the Labenbach into the
floodplain was designed to ensure that during

270
00:26:00,606 --> 00:26:06,027
high water events, the substantial flood load
continues straight through the main channel.

271
00:26:06,027 --> 00:26:12,323
However, a significant amount of water will
also flow into the floodplain via the side

272
00:26:12,335 --> 00:26:18,426
channel, transforming it into a retention
area, specifically a flood retention zone.

273
00:26:20,126 --> 00:26:23,535
For the flood event, bank
protections also had to be

274
00:26:23,547 --> 00:26:27,227
installed in the side channel,
such as this micro groyne.

275
00:26:30,847 --> 00:26:39,923
And the crucial thing is that you can observe that the fish
population in the side channel, even though it is narrower,

276
00:26:39,935 --> 00:26:49,022
is much higher than in the entire section, because even the
fish want to be exactly where the ideal flow conditions are.

277
00:26:52,347 --> 00:26:54,946
And if people want
to enter the floodplain,

278
00:26:54,958 --> 00:26:57,748
the floodplain forest is
accessible to walkers.

279
00:26:57,748 --> 00:27:01,237
Here, they can admire
ancient trees that are rarely

280
00:27:01,249 --> 00:27:04,548
found elsewhere, such
as this magnificent poplar.

281
00:27:08,048 --> 00:27:12,448
Throughout the floodplain, there is
an abundance of diverse tree species.

282
00:27:12,448 --> 00:27:19,618
And ah, these large or mighty trees have been
here since time immemorial; they are not cut

283
00:27:19,630 --> 00:27:27,048
down but left as they are. If they happen to
fall, they naturally turn into fertilizer, right?

284
00:27:27,147 --> 00:27:30,048
This is something we've
managed to preserve.

285
00:27:31,448 --> 00:27:35,248
This is, after all, the last
floodplain on the Große Tullen.

286
00:27:35,448 --> 00:27:39,147
So, this is truly something
unique in this region.

287
00:27:41,647 --> 00:27:48,353
Downstream, numerous micro groynes are directly
connected to the project area. These were installed

288
00:27:48,365 --> 00:27:54,748
on the recommendation of Otmar Grober to enhance
the dynamics and vitality of the main channel.

289
00:27:56,147 --> 00:27:59,389
In the opposite direction,
Christine Sindelar's

290
00:27:59,401 --> 00:28:02,248
meandering ramp is
only 3 kilometers away.

291
00:28:03,847 --> 00:28:11,795
This section of the river, Labenbach Große Tullen in Lower
Austria, serves as a prime example of nature-oriented

292
00:28:11,807 --> 00:28:20,048
river engineering inspired by Viktor Schauberger. Consequently,
it receives the provisional final pin on the IRT map.

293
00:28:21,647 --> 00:28:24,968
The map indicates that
Instream River Training has

294
00:28:24,980 --> 00:28:28,248
predominantly expanded
in areas close to the Alps.

295
00:28:30,147 --> 00:28:34,647
To conclude the IRT hopping, we
head once more to the Black Forest.

296
00:28:34,948 --> 00:28:38,403
Just a short distance from
Offenburg is the town of

297
00:28:38,415 --> 00:28:41,948
Zell, which includes the
district of Unterentersbach.

298
00:28:41,948 --> 00:28:47,990
Since 2001, the local community center there
has been the venue for annual conferences

299
00:28:48,002 --> 00:28:53,847
and exhibitions organized by the Association
for Implosion Research and Application.

300
00:28:53,847 --> 00:28:59,248
The organization seeks to disseminate the
concepts of Viktor and Walter Schauberger.

301
00:29:03,948 --> 00:29:09,465
During the redesign of the village stream,
the association strongly lobbied the local

302
00:29:09,477 --> 00:29:14,748
mayor to implement Instream River Training
techniques for revitalizing the stream.

303
00:29:22,647 --> 00:29:24,647
For example, this snail ramp.

304
00:29:25,048 --> 00:29:28,998
The association's chairman,
Klaus Rauber, successfully

305
00:29:29,010 --> 00:29:33,548
recruited Erich Linzin, a
seasoned IRT expert, for the project.

306
00:29:33,548 --> 00:29:38,891
Even in a small stream, the excavator
operator doesn't merely drop the stones

307
00:29:38,903 --> 00:29:43,847
into the water; instead, they are
carefully embedded into the streambed.

308
00:29:45,248 --> 00:29:48,112
Whether large or
small, the installations

309
00:29:48,124 --> 00:29:51,548
generate a diverse array
of vortices and currents.

310
00:29:51,548 --> 00:29:56,213
This acts as a catalyst for the
natural self-development of the

311
00:29:56,225 --> 00:30:01,048
water body, embodying a core
principle of Instream River Training.

312
00:30:04,587 --> 00:30:08,725
Not far away, Klaus Rauber
gathers with children from his family

313
00:30:08,737 --> 00:30:12,887
and friends for an activity known
as a 'throwing platform' event.

314
00:30:15,887 --> 00:30:18,826
The children place
smaller stones in the water

315
00:30:18,838 --> 00:30:21,788
in such a way that groynes
or ramps are formed.

316
00:30:21,788 --> 00:30:25,267
While playfully building
groynes, the children can

317
00:30:25,279 --> 00:30:29,387
immediately observe how the
water responds to their actions.

318
00:30:35,788 --> 00:30:39,296
Right in the thick of
things is Horst Geiger, who

319
00:30:39,308 --> 00:30:42,688
has organized numerous
throwing platform events.

320
00:30:42,688 --> 00:30:45,788
Now, for the first time, with children.

321
00:30:47,387 --> 00:30:54,825
With children, it's possible at a very
early stage... Children should be introduced

322
00:30:54,837 --> 00:31:01,488
as early as possible to waters and
the river and to building in the river.

323
00:31:02,087 --> 00:31:07,662
Throwing platforms are an effective method
for them to quickly grasp, through firsthand

324
00:31:07,674 --> 00:31:13,387
observation, how strategically placing a few
stones can influence the flow of the current.

325
00:31:17,688 --> 00:31:20,387
The event concludes later in the afternoon.

326
00:31:23,688 --> 00:31:27,762
Of course, the throwing platforms
won’t withstand the water for

327
00:31:27,774 --> 00:31:31,988
long, but perhaps they’ll be
repaired or even rebuilt by children.

328
00:31:39,338 --> 00:31:42,709
My grandfather, Viktor
Schauberger, was captivated

329
00:31:42,721 --> 00:31:46,037
by the movement of
water, particularly the vortex.

330
00:31:46,243 --> 00:31:51,127
My father, Walter Schauberger,
Viktor's son, was a mathematician,

331
00:31:51,139 --> 00:31:55,144
and in his work, the vortex
transformed into a spiral.

332
00:31:56,188 --> 00:32:00,365
It equally symbolizes this
movement from the outside

333
00:32:00,377 --> 00:32:04,487
inward, the concentrating,
coiling system of nature.

334
00:32:24,288 --> 00:32:30,159
At a conference of the Association for
Implosion Research, Anton Sàlat demonstrates how

335
00:32:30,171 --> 00:32:35,988
two distinct types of vortices can be generated
by simply stirring in a water cylinder.

336
00:32:35,988 --> 00:32:41,087
It becomes evident that not all swirling
water exhibits the same properties.

337
00:32:45,188 --> 00:32:49,188
When you stir at the
edge, a rigid vortex forms.

338
00:32:49,188 --> 00:32:53,576
This vortex is centrifugal, meaning
the water particles move faster

339
00:32:53,588 --> 00:32:57,988
on the outside than on the inside,
similar to the motion of a wheel.

340
00:32:57,988 --> 00:33:02,988
An egg resting at the bottom of the
vessel is hurled against the glass wall.

341
00:33:02,988 --> 00:33:07,188
Stirring in the center, however,
generates a potential vortex.

342
00:33:07,188 --> 00:33:11,151
This vortex rotates faster
on the inside than on the

343
00:33:11,163 --> 00:33:15,288
outside, making it centripetal
rather than centrifugal.

344
00:33:15,288 --> 00:33:20,387
In this vortex, the egg is astonishingly
lifted upward against gravity.

345
00:33:20,387 --> 00:33:23,988
Even when you stop stirring,
it initially stays in place.

346
00:33:31,387 --> 00:33:35,288
When ink is added, no
edge vortices are observed.

347
00:33:35,288 --> 00:33:39,788
The egg's rise is solely driven
by the central potential vortex.

348
00:33:43,587 --> 00:33:48,387
So much for a first introduction
to the fascinating world of vortices.

349
00:33:49,988 --> 00:33:54,490
In the PKS Villa near Bad Ischl,
a large model of a hyperbolic

350
00:33:54,502 --> 00:33:58,587
funnel is suspended from
the ceiling in one of the halls.

351
00:33:59,788 --> 00:34:03,570
The funnel is an inverted
hyperbolic cone, formed

352
00:34:03,582 --> 00:34:08,362
geometrically by rotating a
hyperbola around its vertical axis.

353
00:34:12,762 --> 00:34:16,683
Walter Schauberger carried
out numerous vortex experiments

354
00:34:16,695 --> 00:34:19,961
using several of these
funnels in his laboratory.

355
00:34:22,762 --> 00:34:28,461
Here are archival recordings from
1986 of the vortex occurring in the funnel.

356
00:34:31,762 --> 00:34:36,661
In the lower section of the funnel,
an air braid forms into a double helix.

357
00:34:38,987 --> 00:34:43,887
Numerous gas bubbles swirl around
the pulsating braid in helical paths.

358
00:34:47,992 --> 00:34:51,992
Also clearly visible:
the vortex draws in air.

359
00:34:51,992 --> 00:34:55,457
The vortex within the
hyperbolic funnel exhibits

360
00:34:55,469 --> 00:34:58,592
both centrifugal and
centripetal properties.

361
00:35:01,393 --> 00:35:05,846
The funnel that Walter Schauberger
last worked with has become

362
00:35:05,858 --> 00:35:10,393
a popular photo subject during
open house days at the PKS villa.

363
00:35:13,793 --> 00:35:16,815
Visitors take photos
into a mirror, allowing

364
00:35:16,827 --> 00:35:19,793
them to view the swirling
vortex from above.

365
00:35:24,592 --> 00:35:28,886
The vortex is created when
water flows tangentially into the

366
00:35:28,898 --> 00:35:32,992
funnel, immediately transitioning
into a spiraling motion.

367
00:35:32,992 --> 00:35:36,211
Walter's father Viktor
had already referred to

368
00:35:36,223 --> 00:35:39,592
this movement as the
rolling principle of nature.

369
00:35:42,492 --> 00:35:45,878
The vortex in the hyperbolic
funnel forms a large

370
00:35:45,890 --> 00:35:49,492
surface area at the boundaries
between water and air.

371
00:35:50,492 --> 00:35:55,992
This could be utilized, for instance, to
enhance the oxygen content of the water.

372
00:35:57,893 --> 00:36:01,119
A plausible hypothesis
that Jörg Schauberger

373
00:36:01,131 --> 00:36:03,793
aimed to substantiate
scientifically.

374
00:36:03,992 --> 00:36:09,492
To achieve this, he approached a research
institute near the Dutch North Sea coast.

375
00:36:11,893 --> 00:36:15,244
In the capital of the Dutch
province of Friesland,

376
00:36:15,256 --> 00:36:17,893
you'll find the Water
Campus Leeuwarden.

377
00:36:17,893 --> 00:36:23,066
Numerous institutes and companies
conduct research and development here in

378
00:36:23,078 --> 00:36:28,193
the promising field of water technology,
for example the Wetsus Institute.

379
00:36:32,393 --> 00:36:36,224
Here at Wetsus, the European
Center of Excellence for Water

380
00:36:36,236 --> 00:36:40,592
Research, the Schauberger vortex
has been studied for several years.

381
00:36:40,592 --> 00:36:44,536
Bachelor’s theses, master’s
theses, and even doctoral

382
00:36:44,548 --> 00:36:47,992
dissertations have already
been completed here.

383
00:36:47,992 --> 00:36:53,421
Just before this film was completed,
Marten van de Griend earned his Ph.D.

384
00:36:53,433 --> 00:36:59,092
with his research on the theory and
applications of hyperbolic water vortices.

385
00:37:02,492 --> 00:37:06,863
Several times a year, Jörg
Schauberger undertakes the long journey

386
00:37:06,875 --> 00:37:11,193
to Leeuwarden to keep abreast of
the latest research developments.

387
00:37:11,293 --> 00:37:14,128
Initially, the focus
was on measuring the

388
00:37:14,140 --> 00:37:17,393
increase in oxygen levels
in the vortexed water.

389
00:37:20,393 --> 00:37:23,709
For this purpose, measurement
sensors are attached

390
00:37:23,721 --> 00:37:26,592
to the water inlet and
outlet of the funnel.

391
00:37:26,693 --> 00:37:30,893
For the measurements,
low-oxygen groundwater is used.

392
00:37:33,092 --> 00:37:36,593
The funnel experiments
are led by Dr. Elmar Fuchs,

393
00:37:36,605 --> 00:37:40,393
head of the Applied Water
Physics department at Wetzus.

394
00:37:43,693 --> 00:37:49,092
The readings on the laptop screen
clearly indicate a rise in oxygen levels.

395
00:37:50,693 --> 00:37:54,592
Fuchs provides a summary
of the extensive test runs.

396
00:37:56,393 --> 00:38:01,516
The key findings indicate that it's
insufficient to merely allow water to flow

397
00:38:01,528 --> 00:38:06,793
through a hyperbolic funnel, as various
regimes can develop within such a funnel.

398
00:38:09,193 --> 00:38:12,693
These need to be defined,
they need to be properly adjusted.

399
00:38:12,693 --> 00:38:19,959
However, we have demonstrated that in a specific
regime, which we refer to as Twisted Schauberger,

400
00:38:19,971 --> 00:38:26,293
the characteristics of gas transfer into the
liquid phase are exceptionally effective.

401
00:38:58,693 --> 00:39:03,073
The oxygen enrichment within
the funnel is economical during

402
00:39:03,085 --> 00:39:07,693
operation and also aids in the
oxidation of iron in groundwater.

403
00:39:09,992 --> 00:39:13,205
The discussed vortex
regimes can be regulated

404
00:39:13,217 --> 00:39:16,092
by managing the
water inflow and outflow.

405
00:39:16,992 --> 00:39:19,592
At Wetsus, a distinction is made.

406
00:39:20,092 --> 00:39:22,193
Restricted Schauberger.

407
00:39:23,992 --> 00:39:25,693
Straight Schauberger.

408
00:39:26,992 --> 00:39:28,793
And Twisted Schauberger.

409
00:39:29,992 --> 00:39:32,592
There's a profound reason behind this name.

410
00:39:33,092 --> 00:39:39,894
What we are trying to do here is to scientifically
examine, analyze, and uncover the entire legacy of the

411
00:39:39,906 --> 00:39:46,592
three generations of Schauberger—from Viktor Schauberger
through Walter Schauberger to Jörg Schauberger.

412
00:39:48,092 --> 00:39:51,509
What is actually scientifically
measurable here? What can it

413
00:39:51,521 --> 00:39:54,893
be used for? And what advantages
does this technology offer?

414
00:39:55,592 --> 00:40:00,068
And the first step, as mentioned,
was the exploration of the hyperbolic

415
00:40:00,337 --> 00:40:04,693
funnel, with the aim of dissolving
gases in water quickly and efficiently.

416
00:40:08,492 --> 00:40:11,293
The next step has already been taken.

417
00:40:11,293 --> 00:40:15,661
Doctoral candidate Roman
Klimenko is setting up an experiment

418
00:40:15,673 --> 00:40:19,983
that integrates the hyperbolic
funnel with plasma technology.

419
00:40:19,983 --> 00:40:25,384
In a reactor, plasma is created
from specific gases using high voltage.

420
00:40:27,583 --> 00:40:32,391
When this plasma discharges in
the air, new compounds like ozone are

421
00:40:32,403 --> 00:40:37,083
formed, which can oxidize and
neutralize other unwanted substances.

422
00:41:06,013 --> 00:41:14,375
Klimenko's scientific thesis suggests that the likelihood
ozone will oxidize substances such as pharmaceutical residues

423
00:41:14,387 --> 00:41:22,413
and other micro-pollutants in water is significantly enhanced
by the swirling action within the Schauberger funnel.

424
00:41:22,413 --> 00:41:27,983
In practice, numerous hyperbolic copper
funnels, crafted by Klaus Rauber, are

425
00:41:27,995 --> 00:41:33,362
already in daily use, such as in pond
systems, to help reduce algae growth.

426
00:41:35,443 --> 00:41:40,844
Engineer Josef Auernigg has performed
measurements on several copper funnels.

427
00:41:40,844 --> 00:41:45,744
He presents his findings at a
conference of the Implosion Association.

428
00:41:47,443 --> 00:41:53,043
Unlike the Wetsus Institute, Auernigg did
not examine the funnels in the laboratory.

429
00:41:55,344 --> 00:41:59,042
His measurements of oxygen
enrichment in the funnel,

430
00:41:59,054 --> 00:42:03,043
however, show values similar
to those recorded by Wetsus.

431
00:42:05,443 --> 00:42:09,708
In the far north of Germany, between
Husum and Schleswig, there is a

432
00:42:09,720 --> 00:42:14,244
fascinating combination of a constructed
wetland and a hyperbolic funnel.

433
00:42:17,844 --> 00:42:21,247
We have arrived at the
Backensholzer Hof, a farm

434
00:42:21,259 --> 00:42:24,744
with 1,000 cattle that
are allowed to graze daily.

435
00:42:30,144 --> 00:42:33,744
The feed is primarily
cultivated on their own land.

436
00:42:34,244 --> 00:42:37,674
This modern, bio-certified
operation generates

437
00:42:37,686 --> 00:42:40,543
energy through a
large biogas facility.

438
00:42:40,744 --> 00:42:42,844
And on solar panel roofs.

439
00:42:44,443 --> 00:42:49,043
The estate features a farm shop,
a restaurant, and an online store.

440
00:42:55,144 --> 00:42:58,543
The cheese offered here
comes from the own creamery.

441
00:43:01,043 --> 00:43:05,039
At Backensholz, they produce
fifteen different varieties,

442
00:43:05,051 --> 00:43:08,644
amounting to a total of
500 tons of cheese annually.

443
00:43:12,043 --> 00:43:16,244
Nearly 100 people are employed
at the Backensholzer Hof.

444
00:43:16,844 --> 00:43:20,253
However, the volume of
wastewater is equivalent

445
00:43:20,265 --> 00:43:23,543
to that produced by a
community of 800 people.

446
00:43:23,644 --> 00:43:27,844
A portion of the wastewater
is recycled in a pilot facility.

447
00:43:28,344 --> 00:43:33,244
The grease-laden water from the restaurant
kitchen passes through a grease trap.

448
00:43:33,443 --> 00:43:36,714
The toilet water enters a
decomposition chamber,

449
00:43:36,726 --> 00:43:40,344
where the waste settles and
is transformed into humus.

450
00:43:41,744 --> 00:43:44,681
The two pre-treated
wastewater streams then

451
00:43:44,693 --> 00:43:47,844
flow separately through
two hyperbolic funnels.

452
00:43:48,043 --> 00:43:51,844
The wastewater circulates
multiple times through the funnels.

453
00:43:54,244 --> 00:43:59,197
While searching for a natural wastewater
treatment system, my brother and I came across

454
00:43:59,209 --> 00:44:04,344
the hyperbolic funnel, and this funnel, as I
understand it, supplies the water with oxygen.

455
00:44:05,144 --> 00:44:09,152
The water becomes more reactive,
and I can say from experience that

456
00:44:09,164 --> 00:44:13,244
the quality of our wastewater has
become very, very good as a result.

457
00:44:13,744 --> 00:44:17,814
The funnel water is then directed
into a treatment bed, where

458
00:44:17,826 --> 00:44:21,644
specific plants grow to further
enhance the water quality.

459
00:44:24,844 --> 00:44:27,481
What gathers beneath
the constructed

460
00:44:27,493 --> 00:44:30,644
wetland initially
appears to be clear water.

461
00:44:30,844 --> 00:44:34,443
The water is now capable of
being discharged, essentially.

462
00:44:34,443 --> 00:44:38,638
Some of it flows into the pre-flow
system, and depending on our needs, we

463
00:44:38,650 --> 00:44:42,744
extract a portion of this water to
irrigate plants or flush our toilets.

464
00:44:43,844 --> 00:44:47,870
The greenhouse receives
not only recycled water but also

465
00:44:47,882 --> 00:44:52,344
warmth from the composting
chamber located directly beneath it.

466
00:44:53,244 --> 00:44:58,443
All of this demonstrates sustainable
resource management at Backensholzer Hof.

467
00:44:59,744 --> 00:45:03,969
We want to make it possible to use a
resource more than once. And thanks to

468
00:45:03,981 --> 00:45:08,943
Schauberger’s funnel and the plant clarification
bed, that’s exactly what we’ve achieved.

469
00:45:08,943 --> 00:45:12,344
Therefore, we look
optimistically towards the future.

470
00:45:12,744 --> 00:45:17,943
Hyperbolic funnels for water revitalization
are available for home use as well.

471
00:45:17,943 --> 00:45:22,289
The Swiss company Vitor offers a
table funnel, while another version

472
00:45:22,301 --> 00:45:26,344
was introduced to the market by
Jörg Schauberger's wife, Ingrid.

473
00:45:26,543 --> 00:45:32,656
This funnel set, crafted from mouth-blown
glass, solid wood, and copper, is designed

474
00:45:32,668 --> 00:45:38,144
not only to invigorate the water but
also to serve as an eye-catching piece.

475
00:45:40,244 --> 00:45:44,920
It's crucial to me that Walter
Schauberger's years of work not only

476
00:45:44,932 --> 00:45:49,344
remain theoretical but are also
applicable in everyday practice.

477
00:45:52,744 --> 00:45:55,639
The following photographs
were captured at the

478
00:45:55,651 --> 00:45:58,744
Wetsus Institute for Water
Research in Leeuwarden.

479
00:46:00,443 --> 00:46:06,248
They clearly demonstrate that vortices in
small tabletop funnels develop distinct helix

480
00:46:06,260 --> 00:46:12,144
structures, thereby optimizing the contact
surface with the absorbed oxygen from the air.

481
00:46:14,543 --> 00:46:19,344
This gives Ingrid Schauberger even
more reason to drink vortexed water.

482
00:46:19,527 --> 00:46:21,527
It simply tastes fresher.

483
00:46:24,427 --> 00:46:28,983
The concept of a hyperbolic
water vortex device, intended

484
00:46:28,995 --> 00:46:33,327
as a faucet attachment, was
conceived as early as 1972.

485
00:46:33,427 --> 00:46:36,887
At Walter Schauberger’s
suggestion, civil engineer

486
00:46:36,899 --> 00:46:39,827
Wilhelm Martin developed
the vortex device.

487
00:46:39,827 --> 00:46:43,529
In 1980, Jens Fischer
assumed responsibility for

488
00:46:43,541 --> 00:46:47,027
the production and
distribution of the device.

489
00:46:47,527 --> 00:46:52,827
Since then, the original Martin Vortexer
has been sold thousands of times.

490
00:46:52,827 --> 00:46:55,334
Additionally, many
imitation water

491
00:46:55,346 --> 00:46:58,728
revitalization devices
have entered the market.

492
00:47:03,027 --> 00:47:07,235
In the shower, the water from
the vortex drum feels exceptionally

493
00:47:07,247 --> 00:47:10,827
soft, and there's also a
reduction in water consumption.

494
00:47:15,228 --> 00:47:19,369
A hyperbolic vortex device
for industrial applications

495
00:47:19,381 --> 00:47:22,628
was developed in the
Swedish city of Malmö.

496
00:47:25,128 --> 00:47:28,405
In the early 2000s, the
Turning Torso, the new

497
00:47:28,417 --> 00:47:31,427
landmark of the city,
was constructed here.

498
00:47:34,827 --> 00:47:40,567
During this period, a team of Schauberger
researchers, headed by Kurt Hallberg,

499
00:47:40,579 --> 00:47:45,827
was conducting experiments in Malmö
with large hyperbolic vortex nozzles.

500
00:47:45,827 --> 00:47:52,427
These devices allow you to not only
aerate water but also remove gases from it.

501
00:47:52,427 --> 00:47:55,499
The company Watreco
was established, with its

502
00:47:55,511 --> 00:47:58,327
initial clients being
ice rinks in Sweden.

503
00:48:00,427 --> 00:48:05,628
Ice produced from degassed vortex
water is denser, making it more durable.

504
00:48:06,228 --> 00:48:08,927
It lasts longer and conserves energy.

505
00:48:13,128 --> 00:48:22,457
The patent holder was Treco in Malmö, and the distribution company
H2O Vortex in Luxembourg currently supplies not only ice skating

506
00:48:22,469 --> 00:48:31,527
arenas but also industrial facilities, particularly in the
Netherlands, such as in the city of Sevenaar, near the German border.

507
00:48:34,827 --> 00:48:40,836
The Swedish CEO of H2O Vortex, Hakan Grönlund,
along with his Dutch business partner

508
00:48:40,848 --> 00:48:46,728
Mark Bouren, who is the head of the company
Patema, are inspecting a cooling tower.

509
00:48:46,728 --> 00:48:51,327
It is located on the premises of a
large meat and sausage producer.

510
00:48:53,027 --> 00:48:56,915
In the cooling tower, the
water that has heated up during

511
00:48:56,927 --> 00:49:00,827
the plant's air conditioning
process is cooled down again.

512
00:49:02,027 --> 00:49:05,569
Warm water systems
are highly prone to scaling,

513
00:49:05,581 --> 00:49:09,728
corrosion, biofilm formation,
and bacterial infestation.

514
00:49:11,827 --> 00:49:16,728
In traditional cooling systems, these
issues are addressed using chemicals.

515
00:49:20,027 --> 00:49:24,600
A portion of the cooling water
evaporates, but the chemicals stay

516
00:49:24,612 --> 00:49:29,128
in the remaining water, eventually
posing an environmental issue.

517
00:49:31,728 --> 00:49:37,182
Right next to the climate technology hall,
the system outfitter Patema has placed

518
00:49:37,194 --> 00:49:42,327
a container that is directly linked to
the cooling circuit through pipelines.

519
00:49:43,728 --> 00:49:47,997
Inside the container, the cooling
water undergoes a physical

520
00:49:48,009 --> 00:49:51,728
treatment, which removes
the necessity for chemicals.

521
00:49:55,628 --> 00:49:59,316
Suspended above the
pumps and filters is the core of

522
00:49:59,328 --> 00:50:03,027
the system: a hyperbolic
vortex generator by Watreco.

523
00:50:05,228 --> 00:50:08,626
The water enters here
and is swirled around.

524
00:50:09,626 --> 00:50:15,626
Then, small air bubbles are collected
here and separated from the water below.

525
00:50:18,726 --> 00:50:23,827
This results in lower viscosity
and higher density in the water.

526
00:50:26,527 --> 00:50:31,726
In other words, vortexed water is both
more fluid and denser simultaneously.

527
00:50:32,527 --> 00:50:35,427
A vacuum develops at the vortex's core.

528
00:50:36,927 --> 00:50:41,427
Through the vacuum process,
we also crystallize the calcium.

529
00:50:42,927 --> 00:50:48,327
It gets crystallized. This means the
crystals can't adhere inside the pipes.

530
00:50:48,864 --> 00:50:52,536
And furthermore, the
vacuum also kills the bacteria.

531
00:50:53,028 --> 00:50:56,375
This vortex technology
has already demonstrated its

532
00:50:56,387 --> 00:51:00,327
effectiveness in numerous
companies across various countries.

533
00:51:21,842 --> 00:51:24,528
Here's another example
from the Netherlands.

534
00:51:27,289 --> 00:51:31,010
In the port city of Bergen
op Zoom, there is a facility

535
00:51:31,022 --> 00:51:34,289
belonging to a major
potato product manufacturer.

536
00:51:34,590 --> 00:51:36,985
In the food industry,
in particular,

537
00:51:36,997 --> 00:51:39,990
production halls must
be well air-conditioned.

538
00:51:42,390 --> 00:51:47,490
The climate control technology
required for this is cooled using water.

539
00:51:47,490 --> 00:51:52,890
This process demands the equivalent
of 16 swimming pools of water each day.

540
00:51:52,890 --> 00:51:57,090
On the factory hall's roof,
there are two cooling towers.

541
00:51:57,090 --> 00:52:00,708
The water in the cooling
system is treated with two

542
00:52:00,720 --> 00:52:04,490
vortex generators, eliminating
the need for chemicals.

543
00:52:08,590 --> 00:52:11,912
Now we proceed to a
completely different field,

544
00:52:11,924 --> 00:52:15,189
approximately 100
kilometers away in Eindhoven.

545
00:52:18,189 --> 00:52:21,185
The wastewater treatment
plant there, similar to

546
00:52:21,197 --> 00:52:24,390
many others, is increasingly
encountering a problem.

547
00:52:24,390 --> 00:52:28,040
During heavy rainfall, the
treatment basins overflow,

548
00:52:28,052 --> 00:52:32,189
allowing untreated wastewater
to spill into the Dommel River.

549
00:52:32,189 --> 00:52:36,789
This poses a risk of the river
becoming ecologically unstable.

550
00:52:36,789 --> 00:52:42,590
For such an emergency, Patema
and H2O Vortex provided a solution.

551
00:52:42,590 --> 00:52:48,390
These aerators from Wattreco are connected
via hoses to a pumping and control station.

552
00:52:53,890 --> 00:52:59,689
A crane installed 5 modules, each
containing 4 flow mixers, into the Dommel.

553
00:53:01,490 --> 00:53:06,074
When the efficient Flowmixers
activate, they rapidly boost the oxygen

554
00:53:06,086 --> 00:53:10,289
levels in the water, enabling
fish and insect larvae to survive.

555
00:53:16,890 --> 00:53:20,931
Much more affordable than a
large rainwater reservoir that

556
00:53:20,943 --> 00:53:24,789
could refresh the oxygen-depleted
river in an emergency.

557
00:53:25,689 --> 00:53:29,689
The Flowmixers operate based
on a straightforward principle.

558
00:53:30,689 --> 00:53:34,689
Water enters here and is
swirled, creating a vacuum.

559
00:53:36,390 --> 00:53:41,089
This vacuum draws in
air, aerating the water.

560
00:53:42,990 --> 00:53:45,912
It is used in ponds and rivers.

561
00:53:48,490 --> 00:53:52,289
The Flowmixers by Wattreco
have an egg-shaped design.

562
00:53:53,390 --> 00:53:58,390
The next chapter of the film also
focuses on this shape developed by nature.

563
00:54:02,590 --> 00:54:08,515
Undisturbed natural phenomena give
us a clue for designing new technology.

564
00:54:10,789 --> 00:54:15,814
We need to understand
nature to replicate its processes.

565
00:54:33,490 --> 00:54:37,492
For Viktor and Walter
Schauberger, the egg represented

566
00:54:37,504 --> 00:54:40,789
the ideal vessel shape
for natural processes.

567
00:54:40,789 --> 00:54:45,289
Life processes not only begin in
the egg, but are also sustained there.

568
00:54:46,490 --> 00:54:49,792
At the entrance of the
PKS Villa in Bad Ischl,

569
00:54:49,804 --> 00:54:53,189
a large model of a
hyperbolic cone is displayed.

570
00:54:53,189 --> 00:54:56,323
When you slice this cone
at an angle, you obtain

571
00:54:56,335 --> 00:54:59,289
an oval, which is a
two-dimensional egg shape.

572
00:55:01,689 --> 00:55:05,446
Thus, there is also a
mathematical relationship between

573
00:55:05,458 --> 00:55:08,689
the egg shape and the
hyperbolic cone or funnel.

574
00:55:11,189 --> 00:55:15,689
Viktor Schauberger utilized an
egg shape in his noble water device.

575
00:55:16,289 --> 00:55:20,789
Presented here is a replica from the
Association for Implosion Research.

576
00:55:21,090 --> 00:55:27,652
According to Walter Schauberger, as seen in
this 1972 recording, the egg was the perfect

577
00:55:27,664 --> 00:55:34,090
form for technical devices used in the
production of mixtures, solutions, or emulsions.

578
00:55:35,593 --> 00:55:40,300
Schauberger commissioned the construction
of a large egg-shaped reactor for

579
00:55:40,312 --> 00:55:45,093
hands-on experiments, but financial
constraints brought the trials to an end.

580
00:55:48,894 --> 00:55:54,614
At a conference hosted by the Implosion
Association, Anton Sàlat presents a replica

581
00:55:54,626 --> 00:56:00,494
of a water purification device that can be
directly traced back to Viktor Schauberger.

582
00:56:00,494 --> 00:56:04,416
Inside the egg-shaped interior,
a stirring mechanism at the

583
00:56:04,428 --> 00:56:08,494
bottom of the vessel generates
a captivating potential vortex.

584
00:56:44,393 --> 00:56:51,930
We observe a distinct difference between the potential
vortex and what outflow vortices represent, as the outflow

585
00:56:51,942 --> 00:56:59,094
vortex still contains centrifugal elements, whereas the
potential vortex is distinguished by new properties.

586
00:57:00,743 --> 00:57:06,497
The potential vortex exhibits
characteristics such as structure formation,

587
00:57:06,509 --> 00:57:12,044
the creation of individual veil
layers, pulsation, and swelling effects.

588
00:57:12,643 --> 00:57:15,418
This technique is utilized
by the pharmaceutical

589
00:57:15,430 --> 00:57:18,444
company WALA to blend
two different mistletoe juices.

590
00:57:24,144 --> 00:57:26,958
WALA Heilmittel is
a foundation-owned

591
00:57:26,970 --> 00:57:30,243
company located in
Bad Boll, near Stuttgart.

592
00:57:30,343 --> 00:57:38,722
Since 1935, the company has been producing naturally
effective medicines under the guiding principle

593
00:57:38,734 --> 00:57:46,543
'From Nature for Humanity,' and since 1967, WALA
produces also Dr. Hauschka natural cosmetics.

594
00:57:48,244 --> 00:57:53,844
Through rhythmic manufacturing processes,
products are created that benefit health.

595
00:57:55,543 --> 00:58:00,286
The foundation for this is formed by
natural substances, which are sourced

596
00:58:00,298 --> 00:58:05,244
from organic farming whenever possible
and are obtained under fair conditions.

597
00:58:08,344 --> 00:58:12,744
Today, the company exports
products to around 40 countries.

598
00:58:16,244 --> 00:58:20,564
In pharmaceutical manufacturing,
a specially designed apparatus

599
00:58:20,576 --> 00:58:25,044
is used, among other things, to
mix the plant juices of mistletoe.

600
00:58:28,943 --> 00:58:34,743
In 1976, the predecessor model of
this apparatus was transferred to WALA.

601
00:58:36,743 --> 00:58:40,243
The WALA archivist
knows the entire history.

602
00:58:41,303 --> 00:58:47,883
In the 1920s, Rudolf Steiner provided inspiration
for the development of anthroposophically extended

603
00:58:47,895 --> 00:58:54,943
medicine and pharmacy, suggesting the production of
medicines from mistletoe using a special mixing process.

604
00:58:55,142 --> 00:58:57,663
An extract from winter
mistletoe was to be

605
00:58:57,675 --> 00:59:00,443
combined with an extract
from summer mistletoe.

606
00:59:01,993 --> 00:59:05,861
It was noted that the extracts
should circulate within the drop.

607
00:59:06,443 --> 00:59:10,243
A rudimentary sketch of this can be
seen in Steiner's blackboard drawing.

608
00:59:12,644 --> 00:59:17,544
The anthroposophical physician Karl Köller
was familiar with Rudolf Steiner's ideas.

609
00:59:17,762 --> 00:59:21,960
In the 1960s, he felt compelled
to implement these through a

610
00:59:21,972 --> 00:59:26,043
mixing process different from
those known up to that point.

611
00:59:26,044 --> 00:59:31,246
An earlier meeting with Viktor Schauberger
and his suggestion to move the liquid

612
00:59:31,258 --> 00:59:36,343
rather than the container led to the
construction of Köller's mixing apparatus.

613
00:59:38,544 --> 00:59:43,289
It bears a strong resemblance to the
noble water devices developed by the

614
00:59:43,301 --> 00:59:48,444
Association for Implosion Research, which
also trace back to Viktor Schauberger.

615
00:59:49,444 --> 00:59:53,170
Inside the egg-shaped
interior, a vortex rotates, and the

616
00:59:53,182 --> 00:59:57,243
resulting image is reminiscent
of Steiner's blackboard drawing.

617
01:00:00,343 --> 01:00:05,644
A visualization of what Steiner might have
meant by 'circulating within the drop.'

618
01:00:05,944 --> 01:00:10,880
In 2011, the original Köller
apparatus was reconstructed according

619
01:00:10,892 --> 01:00:15,544
to modern standards and has
been used in production ever since.

620
01:00:15,544 --> 01:00:18,644
The apparatus consists
of an egg-shaped vessel.

621
01:00:20,544 --> 01:00:25,595
The ice tip consists of a silver-plated
copper bowl with a stirring mechanism at

622
01:00:25,607 --> 01:00:30,544
the bottom, above it a transparent dome,
topped with a gold-plated copper bowl.

623
01:00:33,243 --> 01:00:36,843
This apparatus is used for
mixing mistletoe extracts.

624
01:00:36,843 --> 01:00:42,243
For this purpose, mistletoe plants are
harvested by a wild collector for WALA.

625
01:00:43,644 --> 01:00:46,768
Both male and female
plants are cut down to

626
01:00:46,780 --> 01:00:50,343
ensure a balanced mixing
ratio in the formulation.

627
01:00:57,743 --> 01:01:00,939
Immediately after
harvesting, the plants are

628
01:01:00,951 --> 01:01:04,444
carefully dried and
processed directly as needed.

629
01:01:06,743 --> 01:01:09,972
After pressing the extracts
from the dried mistletoe,

630
01:01:09,984 --> 01:01:13,044
the clarified extracts are
mixed in this apparatus.

631
01:01:16,644 --> 01:01:19,028
For this purpose,
the extract from the

632
01:01:19,040 --> 01:01:21,743
winter mistletoe is
added to the lower bowl.

633
01:01:28,144 --> 01:01:29,644
The hood is put on.

634
01:01:30,743 --> 01:01:32,944
And the upper bowl is fixed.

635
01:01:36,144 --> 01:01:41,644
Then the compressed air guide is set in
rotation, creating a vortex in the extract.

636
01:01:44,944 --> 01:01:48,498
Now the extract from the
summer mistletoe is poured into the

637
01:01:48,510 --> 01:01:51,843
upper bowl and begins to drip
downward through the spout.

638
01:01:54,043 --> 01:01:58,944
The drops enter the vortex and merge
with the extract from the winter mistletoe.

639
01:02:01,843 --> 01:02:06,144
Before delivery, additional
processing steps are necessary.

640
01:02:06,944 --> 01:02:10,376
After a final filtration
step, the mixture is diluted

641
01:02:10,388 --> 01:02:13,768
in precise proportions,
then bottled and distributed.

642
01:02:16,644 --> 01:02:20,596
In collaboration with the
Association for Implosion Research,

643
01:02:20,608 --> 01:02:24,444
the understanding of the WALA
mixing apparatus was deepened.

644
01:02:25,843 --> 01:02:29,952
The similarity between Köller's
apparatus from WALA and a

645
01:02:29,964 --> 01:02:33,942
noble water device of the
association is quite apparent.

646
01:02:33,942 --> 01:02:36,891
Both devices implement
similar technical

647
01:02:36,903 --> 01:02:40,442
principles, adapted to
their respective purposes.

648
01:02:46,942 --> 01:02:51,815
In the workshop of the Implosion
Association, next to the replicas of

649
01:02:51,827 --> 01:02:56,643
Viktor Schauberger’s noble water
device, stands a glass egg vortexer.

650
01:02:57,143 --> 01:02:59,942
Anton Sàlat is preparing an experiment.

651
01:03:00,543 --> 01:03:05,442
The glass egg also contains a stirring
mechanism in the bottom of the vessel.

652
01:03:05,442 --> 01:03:10,742
The stirrer initiates a vortex phenomenon,
just as in the Schauberger replicas.

653
01:03:11,242 --> 01:03:14,237
We observe movements
and impulses here that cannot

654
01:03:14,249 --> 01:03:16,843
be explained by the
stirring activity alone.

655
01:03:20,643 --> 01:03:23,722
Viktor Schauberger calls
such a movement mediate, in

656
01:03:23,734 --> 01:03:26,942
contrast to the immediate,
directly triggered movement.

657
01:03:28,543 --> 01:03:31,741
He describes that
constructive and life-giving

658
01:03:31,753 --> 01:03:35,442
natural forces are at work
in such indirect movements.

659
01:03:36,143 --> 01:03:39,968
Only the ideal interplay
of vessel shape, stirrer,

660
01:03:39,980 --> 01:03:44,043
speed, and vortex creates
such fascinating pulsations.

661
01:03:46,043 --> 01:03:49,616
Since vortices are ubiquitous
in nature, this research

662
01:03:49,628 --> 01:03:53,343
helps us better understand
and harness natural processes.

663
01:03:54,543 --> 01:03:57,677
Behind the association's
workshop, Klaus Rauber

664
01:03:57,689 --> 01:04:00,442
has set up another
fundamental experiment.

665
01:04:01,643 --> 01:04:06,643
In particular, this is about the
behavior of carbonic acid in water.

666
01:04:08,942 --> 01:04:12,023
Many are familiar with the
carbonation process, where

667
01:04:12,035 --> 01:04:15,242
carbon dioxide is forced into
water under high pressure.

668
01:04:15,343 --> 01:04:18,519
If you leave this sparkling
water in an open glass

669
01:04:18,531 --> 01:04:21,843
for a while, the carbon
dioxide escapes very quickly.

670
01:04:21,843 --> 01:04:25,089
However, if you incorporate
carbon dioxide using a

671
01:04:25,101 --> 01:04:28,742
vortex process, the situation
could look quite different.

672
01:04:28,742 --> 01:04:32,464
For this purpose, we have
chosen this hyperbolic copper

673
01:04:32,476 --> 01:04:36,543
funnel, which operates as a
rotating body powered by a motor.

674
01:04:36,643 --> 01:04:41,742
These tubes are integrated into
its wall, each ending with a nozzle.

675
01:04:43,442 --> 01:04:48,043
This rotor is suspended upside
down in a vat partially filled with water.

676
01:04:48,843 --> 01:04:51,942
The mouth of the funnel
is submerged underwater.

677
01:04:53,143 --> 01:04:56,329
When the funnel begins
to rotate, water is drawn

678
01:04:56,341 --> 01:04:59,343
in and glides upward
due to centrifugal force.

679
01:05:03,343 --> 01:05:07,546
The water rises along the
inner surface of this funnel wall

680
01:05:07,558 --> 01:05:11,843
as a thin film, then collects
inside this cylindrical region.

681
01:05:12,843 --> 01:05:15,942
And is propelled outward
through the pipes and nozzles.

682
01:05:19,942 --> 01:05:24,143
Then CO₂ is injected into the
mouth of the funnel from below.

683
01:05:25,442 --> 01:05:30,690
Since this gas is heavier than air but
lighter than water, it fills the interior

684
01:05:30,702 --> 01:05:35,442
of the funnel from bottom to top,
displacing the air through the nozzles.

685
01:05:39,643 --> 01:05:44,543
The advantage of this arrangement is
the large surface area of the water film.

686
01:05:44,543 --> 01:05:49,643
This allows a large amount of CO₂ to be
dissolved in the water without pressure.

687
01:05:50,242 --> 01:05:53,649
The water is collected at
the periphery of the vat and

688
01:05:53,661 --> 01:05:57,143
flows back through the water
meter into the intake area.

689
01:05:57,743 --> 01:06:01,322
In this experimental setup,
the water film remains

690
01:06:01,334 --> 01:06:04,643
in motion under a CO₂
atmosphere for some time.

691
01:06:06,442 --> 01:06:11,497
It will now be interesting to see how
long the CO₂ can remain bound in the water,

692
01:06:11,509 --> 01:06:16,143
allowing it to be used, for example,
as a liquid fertilizer in greenhouses.

693
01:06:16,143 --> 01:06:18,760
The plants could absorb
the CO₂ directly from the

694
01:06:18,772 --> 01:06:21,243
water. It wouldn’t need
to escape into the air.

695
01:06:22,942 --> 01:06:27,243
We hope to obtain answers to these
questions through this experimental setup.

696
01:06:28,143 --> 01:06:32,161
For many years, there has been
close collaboration between the

697
01:06:32,173 --> 01:06:36,842
Association for Implosion Research
and the Swiss scientist Felix Hediger.

698
01:06:36,842 --> 01:06:39,543
He is both an artist and an engineer.

699
01:06:40,342 --> 01:06:44,397
Hediger has developed a pump
that incorporates concepts from

700
01:06:44,409 --> 01:06:48,543
Viktor Schauberger and
Serbian-American inventor Nikola Tesla.

701
01:06:49,342 --> 01:06:53,043
Hediger therefore calls
his pump the STH pump.

702
01:06:54,543 --> 01:06:59,442
In test runs, it has already achieved
a pumping height of 20 meters.

703
01:06:59,543 --> 01:07:03,143
We are examining a pump in this test setup.

704
01:07:04,342 --> 01:07:09,996
It has a relatively unique design. I
developed the concept years ago, inspired by

705
01:07:10,008 --> 01:07:15,743
Viktor Schauberger's ideas, to transport
water in a way that is natural and gentle.

706
01:07:17,543 --> 01:07:21,554
The water flows in a
spiral, snail-like pattern into

707
01:07:21,566 --> 01:07:25,437
the rotor and exits
tangentially through the vanes.

708
01:07:25,836 --> 01:07:28,471
The water is not
shattered by paddles, but

709
01:07:28,483 --> 01:07:31,437
instead transported
through the use of friction.

710
01:07:31,437 --> 01:07:36,689
That's almost paradoxical, because
friction is normally always avoided in

711
01:07:36,701 --> 01:07:42,037
engineering applications, yet here it
actually serves as the driving force.

712
01:07:43,137 --> 01:07:50,433
So, it's quite similar to Viktor Schauberger's
approach to technical problems, where he maintained

713
01:07:50,445 --> 01:07:57,236
that one simply needs to think in the completely
opposite direction—180 degrees differently.

714
01:07:57,236 --> 01:08:01,575
Hediger designed the pump
using CAD software and produced

715
01:08:01,587 --> 01:08:05,937
the snail-shaped or spiral-formed
rotor with a 3D printer.

716
01:08:13,236 --> 01:08:16,865
A waterproof endoscopic
camera, inserted into the

717
01:08:16,877 --> 01:08:20,736
outlet pipe, shows the
rotating pump rotor in action.

718
01:08:22,637 --> 01:08:27,336
Hediger's construction goes far beyond
the conventional engineering approach.

719
01:08:27,437 --> 01:08:31,437
It's about technology that
follows the principles of nature.

720
01:08:31,437 --> 01:08:34,631
What this pump topic
touches on is actually a

721
01:08:34,643 --> 01:08:38,336
general theme that Viktor
Schauberger also addressed.

722
01:08:42,637 --> 01:08:45,970
I would say it’s a
paradigm shift approaching

723
01:08:45,982 --> 01:08:49,836
society, namely putting
living systems at the center.

724
01:08:50,937 --> 01:08:56,206
He called what he pursued biotechnology.
Of course, today we understand something a

725
01:08:56,218 --> 01:09:01,437
bit different by biotechnology, but he
definitely put living systems at the center.

726
01:09:04,937 --> 01:09:13,160
And that's exactly what's happening here: I'm trying to place
principles or laws of life—drawing on Schauberger's ideas—at the

727
01:09:13,172 --> 01:09:21,537
very heart of this pump. That means this rotor is shaped according
to the laws of life, and also possesses a certain flexibility.

728
01:09:28,736 --> 01:09:32,036
It's elastic, which
allows it to transport

729
01:09:32,048 --> 01:09:35,437
water in a very gentle,
non-destructive way.

730
01:09:39,336 --> 01:09:42,745
During the pump tests, the
flow rate, the pressure in the

731
01:09:42,757 --> 01:09:46,236
water column, and the power
of the pump motor are measured.

732
01:09:49,537 --> 01:09:53,236
Manual measurements
serve as additional verification.

733
01:09:54,836 --> 01:09:59,137
In the end, what emerges is what's
known as a pump performance curve.

734
01:10:00,937 --> 01:10:14,632
You can see from this pump characteristic curve that this pump has technical potential, even though
its main development focus isn't on creating a high-performance pump or achieving particularly good

735
01:10:14,644 --> 01:10:29,037
efficiency. Instead, it aims to enable an especially gentle form of fluid transport or even to enhance the
water quality with this pump design, with completely different considerations playing the primary role.

736
01:10:35,937 --> 01:10:40,336
Despite these considerations, you
can see that it is technically capable.

737
01:10:43,037 --> 01:10:45,437
Competition stimulates business.

738
01:10:45,637 --> 01:10:51,137
An alternative to the STH pump, depending
on the application, is the whorlpipe.

739
01:10:52,236 --> 01:10:55,694
Its wound riser pipes in
the form of a spiral can

740
01:10:55,706 --> 01:10:59,037
not only transport water
but also distribute it.

741
01:11:00,937 --> 01:11:05,937
A visit to aircraft design engineer
Jan van Thor in the Netherlands.

742
01:11:06,336 --> 01:11:09,499
Jörg Schauberger has
brought an original whorlpipe

743
01:11:09,511 --> 01:11:12,437
invented by his grandfather
Viktor Schauberger.

744
01:11:12,437 --> 01:11:16,836
Immersed like this and then rotated,
and then it sprays out at the top.

745
01:11:18,037 --> 01:11:19,637
And with yours, it is too.

746
01:11:19,836 --> 01:11:20,836
Exactly.

747
01:11:21,037 --> 01:11:24,736
I tried to recreate it just
as Viktor did in the original.

748
01:11:27,437 --> 01:11:32,033
Jan van Thor has produced
several variants using 3D printers to

749
01:11:32,045 --> 01:11:36,437
optimize the whorlpipe through
a process spanning many years.

750
01:11:37,336 --> 01:11:41,921
Van Thor had the efficiency of
his whorlpipes scientifically tested at

751
01:11:41,933 --> 01:11:46,336
the Water Campus Leeuwarden,
which is natural for him as a Dutchman.

752
01:11:46,537 --> 01:11:50,287
The energy savings
achieved through the whorlpipe

753
01:11:50,299 --> 01:11:54,137
in water transfer applications
is 60 to 70 percent.

754
01:11:57,336 --> 01:12:02,037
This was subsequently verified at
the Water Campus in Leeuwarden.

755
01:12:02,637 --> 01:12:06,581
The whorlpipe variant shown
here moves 4 cubic meters

756
01:12:06,593 --> 01:12:10,037
of water per hour at 15
revolutions per second.

757
01:12:12,836 --> 01:12:16,937
The drive motor requires
only 45 watts for this.

758
01:12:16,937 --> 01:12:23,378
Van Thor envisions numerous applications,
such as water aeration, but also the treatment

759
01:12:23,390 --> 01:12:29,336
of liquid manure and implementation in
greenhouses and water treatment facilities.

760
01:12:29,336 --> 01:12:31,236
Viktor Schauberger once said.

761
01:12:33,537 --> 01:12:34,437
We...

762
01:12:35,137 --> 01:12:39,836
...compress water far too much,
which is detrimental to the water.

763
01:12:41,037 --> 01:12:45,775
And with pressure, you need
many more pressure-related devices,

764
01:12:45,787 --> 01:12:50,537
you need much more energy
than if a medium flows into a suction.

765
01:12:50,537 --> 01:13:01,485
And that was also the principle behind his whorlpipe. He said it has
to be drawn in by suction. As a result, you naturally need much less

766
01:13:01,497 --> 01:13:12,537
energy and it's far more efficient to use such devices wherever strong
water flow or water movement is needed, for example in fish farming.

767
01:13:12,736 --> 01:13:16,717
The potential energy savings
would be enormous if water could

768
01:13:16,729 --> 01:13:20,336
be moved using whorlpipes
and other Schauberger devices.

769
01:13:26,162 --> 01:13:31,141
The soil that is expected to yield a
good harvest must be continuously

770
01:13:31,153 --> 01:13:35,862
supplied with substances that
plants require for their development.

771
01:13:36,336 --> 01:13:42,298
The mediator and carrier of these substances
is groundwater, which in its internal

772
01:13:42,310 --> 01:13:47,637
cycle constantly brings fresh nutrient
salts up from the earth's interior.

773
01:13:48,177 --> 01:13:53,177
If the groundwater level drops, the
natural supply of nutrients ceases.

774
01:13:56,576 --> 01:13:58,976
About the forest and the water.

775
01:14:00,376 --> 01:14:02,677
The forest is the cradle of water.

776
01:14:03,177 --> 01:14:10,080
If the forest dies, then the springs dry up,
the fields become barren, and a restlessness

777
01:14:10,092 --> 01:14:16,777
must emerge on Earth that we can all
perceive—more intensely than is beneficial for us.

778
01:14:18,177 --> 01:14:23,677
Viktor Schauberger from his
essay 'The Dying Forest' (1936).

779
01:14:40,652 --> 01:14:44,251
Garden tools are shipped all
over the world from Bad Ischl.

780
01:14:46,051 --> 01:14:51,051
These are garden tools that are handcrafted
and made from bronze, a copper alloy.

781
01:14:54,652 --> 01:14:58,366
Viktor and Walter Schauberger
were granted several

782
01:14:58,378 --> 01:15:02,251
patents for copper-based
soil cultivation implements.

783
01:15:02,251 --> 01:15:06,429
The trace elements contained
in copper or copper alloys are

784
01:15:06,441 --> 01:15:11,051
intended to be introduced directly
into the soil through abrasion.

785
01:15:12,477 --> 01:15:16,311
The company Kupferspuren,
founded in 1995, sells its

786
01:15:16,323 --> 01:15:20,676
products through distribution
partners and via the internet.

787
01:15:20,876 --> 01:15:25,376
Our customers come from Europe,
North America, and Eastern Europe.

788
01:15:28,576 --> 01:15:32,620
Japan, New Zealand, Australia,
and sometimes we receive

789
01:15:32,632 --> 01:15:36,977
special, truly exceptional
requests, for example from Spain.

790
01:15:41,076 --> 01:15:47,671
This particular inquiry came from southern
Spain, from Andalusia. Located about an hour's

791
01:15:47,683 --> 01:15:53,777
drive from the port city of Málaga, lies
the picturesque town of Valle de Abdalaís.

792
01:15:53,777 --> 01:15:59,177
The place is certainly not a gateway to the
Garden of Eden, as for years here, like in

793
01:15:59,189 --> 01:16:04,477
many parts of Spain, the water levels of
the reservoirs have been steadily declining.

794
01:16:05,376 --> 01:16:11,926
In Valle de Abdalaís, the situation is compounded by
the fact that the construction of a high-speed rail line

795
01:16:11,938 --> 01:16:18,977
destroyed a natural water reservoir, which has now significantly
complicated the drinking water supply for the region.

796
01:16:21,076 --> 01:16:24,408
In this drought-prone
region, olive cultivation

797
01:16:24,420 --> 01:16:27,277
represents a significant
economic factor.

798
01:16:27,676 --> 01:16:29,676
Olive groves are everywhere.

799
01:16:30,676 --> 01:16:33,376
On steep slopes and barren soils.

800
01:16:36,777 --> 01:16:41,777
Typically, olive farmers cultivate the
soil several times throughout the year.

801
01:16:43,576 --> 01:16:47,477
They typically don't use a
plow, but rather a cultivator.

802
01:16:52,376 --> 01:16:55,676
The cultivator loosens the
soil without turning it over.

803
01:16:58,076 --> 01:17:03,076
But the olive farmers primarily
use the cultivator for weed control.

804
01:17:08,785 --> 01:17:13,684
On this abandoned plot, the last
time it was tilled was two years ago.

805
01:17:14,384 --> 01:17:19,884
Vegetation barely grows here, but many
olive farmers still find this insufficient.

806
01:17:22,785 --> 01:17:27,573
With chemical herbicides, they eliminate
all plants between the olive trees,

807
01:17:27,585 --> 01:17:32,384
because conventional olive farmers
view weeds as competition for their trees.

808
01:17:34,684 --> 01:17:38,237
However, entirely in the
spirit of Viktor Schauberger,

809
01:17:38,249 --> 01:17:41,684
one can also approach it
in exactly the opposite way.

810
01:17:41,684 --> 01:17:45,134
In 2011, Josef Leiner,
who comes from Austria but

811
01:17:45,146 --> 01:17:48,884
grew up here in Andalusia,
purchased this olive grove.

812
01:17:50,285 --> 01:17:53,123
Since then, he has
switched to permaculture

813
01:17:53,135 --> 01:17:55,984
and completely forgoes
the use of chemicals.

814
01:17:56,484 --> 01:18:00,984
Unlike conventional farming, for us
these aren't weeds, but companion plants.

815
01:18:01,285 --> 01:18:06,085
They enter into a mutual symbiosis and
thereby generate fertilizer and moisture.

816
01:18:06,085 --> 01:18:08,497
Here, for example, we
have marguerites, and you

817
01:18:08,509 --> 01:18:10,984
can see all the biodiversity
that's growing here.

818
01:18:10,984 --> 01:18:13,785
We don't eradicate anything,
we let everything grow.

819
01:18:14,085 --> 01:18:17,884
And the best representative of
our permaculture is the fennel.

820
01:18:18,884 --> 01:18:20,785
It grows here under the trees.

821
01:18:20,785 --> 01:18:23,184
We simply let it grow as well.

822
01:18:23,184 --> 01:18:25,085
It blooms, the olive tree blooms.

823
01:18:25,085 --> 01:18:28,285
They provide each other
with fertilizer and moisture.

824
01:18:28,285 --> 01:18:32,373
Especially during the harsh summer,
when temperatures soar above

825
01:18:32,385 --> 01:18:36,484
45 degrees Celsius (113 Fahrenheit),
they continue to bloom here.

826
01:18:36,484 --> 01:18:39,684
And that's how they survive
the summer without any irrigation.

827
01:18:39,684 --> 01:18:43,984
There is also a very aromatic
oil and a very aromatic fennel.

828
01:18:43,984 --> 01:18:47,684
Even for permaculture,
there's no rule without exception.

829
01:18:48,085 --> 01:18:52,085
Of course, there are also
weeds with negative properties.

830
01:18:52,085 --> 01:18:54,085
Here we have the wild pistachio.

831
01:18:54,384 --> 01:18:56,384
This is the opposite of symbiosis.

832
01:18:56,384 --> 01:18:58,484
It competes with the tree.

833
01:18:58,484 --> 01:19:01,085
You can see, it grows very vigorously here.

834
01:19:01,085 --> 01:19:03,585
And it draws the sap away from the tree.

835
01:19:03,585 --> 01:19:06,984
It hardly produces any
blossoms and barely grows.

836
01:19:06,984 --> 01:19:09,785
We remove these manually over time.

837
01:19:12,085 --> 01:19:14,884
Leiner's olive grove covers 12 hectares.

838
01:19:15,484 --> 01:19:19,785
Occasionally, he fertilizes
the soil with stable manure.

839
01:19:19,785 --> 01:19:23,785
For example, from these goats
belonging to a neighboring farm.

840
01:19:30,085 --> 01:19:34,684
Today, Josef Leiner is driving
to the farm for a different reason.

841
01:19:39,484 --> 01:19:42,884
Once a year, he borrows
a cultivator from the farmer.

842
01:19:43,484 --> 01:19:47,684
However, before it's put to
use, a conversion is performed.

843
01:19:50,184 --> 01:19:53,028
After the steel tines
of the cultivator are

844
01:19:53,040 --> 01:19:56,285
unscrewed, Leiner replaces
them with bronze tines.

845
01:19:58,585 --> 01:20:03,384
The blanks for these tines came from
the copper equipment shop in Bad Ischl.

846
01:20:07,984 --> 01:20:11,285
Leiner then had them
bent into shape on site.

847
01:20:12,484 --> 01:20:18,085
But how did Leiner come up with the idea of
equipping the cultivator with bronze tines?

848
01:20:19,585 --> 01:20:22,102
I read about Viktor
Schauberger on the internet

849
01:20:22,114 --> 01:20:24,484
and then went through
the implosion journals.

850
01:20:26,184 --> 01:20:34,443
There I came across the story of how Viktor Schauberger discovered in
Bulgaria that steel plows dry out the soil due to rust formation that

851
01:20:34,455 --> 01:20:42,785
gets incorporated into the soil, and that areas still cultivated with
wooden plows produced higher yields than those worked with steel plows.

852
01:20:48,184 --> 01:20:49,884
Afterwards, he then...

853
01:20:50,585 --> 01:20:51,984
...discovered that with...

854
01:20:55,484 --> 01:20:57,085
...Copper,...

855
01:20:57,085 --> 01:21:00,585
You can improve the soil even
more and achieve even higher yields.

856
01:21:01,384 --> 01:21:05,285
In this case, we have bronze,
which is an alloy of copper and tin.

857
01:21:05,884 --> 01:21:08,484
It's completely free of oxidation.

858
01:21:11,884 --> 01:21:17,316
Viktor's knowledge of Bulgaria and
the results of his own field experiments

859
01:21:17,328 --> 01:21:21,984
with copper devices date from
the first half of the last century.

860
01:21:23,684 --> 01:21:27,684
The plow was then called the golden
plow because crop yields increased.

861
01:21:29,184 --> 01:21:31,873
And we've adapted that
to the cultivator design

862
01:21:31,885 --> 01:21:34,585
and now implement it
with the bronze cultivator.

863
01:21:35,884 --> 01:21:39,184
Leiner's cultivator has 11 bronze tines.

864
01:21:39,384 --> 01:21:42,675
Compared to steel tines,
they penetrate the soil much

865
01:21:42,687 --> 01:21:45,684
more easily and break
down the earth more finely.

866
01:21:49,384 --> 01:21:54,484
This smooth operation results in the
tractor consuming significantly less fuel.

867
01:21:56,184 --> 01:21:59,997
The abrasion from the bronze
tines doesn't bring decomposing

868
01:22:00,009 --> 01:22:04,085
rust residues into the soil, but
rather enriching trace elements.

869
01:22:07,684 --> 01:22:12,575
Since switching to permaculture,
Leiner only tills his olive grove once

870
01:22:12,587 --> 01:22:17,285
a year to make it possible to lay
out nets during the harvest season.

871
01:22:22,085 --> 01:22:26,524
Despite the relatively infrequent
use of the cultivator, the bronze

872
01:22:26,536 --> 01:22:31,184
tines have had a positive effect on
the soil's water balance over time.

873
01:22:34,085 --> 01:22:37,785
This field has been cultivated with
the bronze cultivator for years now.

874
01:22:37,785 --> 01:22:40,785
You can see everything
is growing green and lush.

875
01:22:40,785 --> 01:22:45,785
That's a sign that moisture is present, and
the tree also benefits from this moisture.

876
01:22:45,785 --> 01:22:49,411
On the other side of the
path, iron was used for digging.

877
01:22:49,411 --> 01:22:52,310
You can see here that the
water balance is different.

878
01:22:52,911 --> 01:22:55,810
It's a different shade of
green, everything is drier.

879
01:22:55,810 --> 01:22:58,569
You can see dried
herbs, and the tree doesn't

880
01:22:58,581 --> 01:23:01,111
grow or bloom as
vigorously as the others.

881
01:23:03,510 --> 01:23:08,655
It was the positive effects of the
bronze cultivator that first encouraged

882
01:23:08,667 --> 01:23:13,411
Josef Leiner to transform his olive
grove into a permaculture system.

883
01:23:17,911 --> 01:23:21,711
He also found a suitable
oil mill in a neighboring town.

884
01:23:24,911 --> 01:23:27,933
In this facility, the
olives are pressed purely

885
01:23:27,945 --> 01:23:31,611
mechanically, that is, without
chemical extraction agents.

886
01:23:34,211 --> 01:23:38,611
After a brief intermediate
storage, the bottling process begins.

887
01:23:40,911 --> 01:23:45,083
Leiner's sweet-tasting olive
oil is marketed under the brand

888
01:23:45,095 --> 01:23:49,211
name Goldpflug on the internet
and sold by select retailers.

889
01:23:54,911 --> 01:23:59,310
From Spain back to Austria,
from agriculture to forestry.

890
01:24:00,810 --> 01:24:05,089
Already 100 years ago, Viktor
Schauberger warned of forest

891
01:24:05,101 --> 01:24:09,611
decline, caused for example by
monoculture forestry practices.

892
01:24:12,810 --> 01:24:16,611
Matthias Pointinger is a
fifth-generation trained forester.

893
01:24:18,310 --> 01:24:23,111
He knows all too well that spruce
monocultures are still being planted today.

894
01:24:24,411 --> 01:24:27,810
This one in Styria is about 40 years old.

895
01:24:27,810 --> 01:24:33,310
Upon entering, even a layperson will
notice the sparsely vegetated forest floor.

896
01:24:33,310 --> 01:24:36,877
Behind me, this is what I
consider a typical monoculture that

897
01:24:36,889 --> 01:24:40,411
was essentially established
after Viktor Schauberger's death.

898
01:24:40,411 --> 01:24:43,111
You can see that nothing
has been learned from this.

899
01:24:43,111 --> 01:24:46,111
Once again, human
profit simply takes priority.

900
01:24:46,111 --> 01:24:49,211
Yes, people are willing to
accept all the consequences.

901
01:24:49,211 --> 01:24:52,437
We know that spruce trees
have shallow root systems; they're

902
01:24:52,449 --> 01:24:56,111
extremely unstable in high winds,
storms, and under heavy snow loads.

903
01:24:56,111 --> 01:24:59,611
Naturally, one wonders:
Why do people do it this way?

904
01:24:59,611 --> 01:25:01,810
And the reason is quite simple.

905
01:25:01,810 --> 01:25:04,919
It's still being taught that
the spruce monoculture is the

906
01:25:04,931 --> 01:25:08,211
'bread tree' of forestry—the
forester's main source of income.

907
01:25:08,211 --> 01:25:12,238
When entering a mixed cultivation,
as opposed to a monoculture,

908
01:25:12,250 --> 01:25:15,911
you essentially have a green
carpet rolled out before you.

909
01:25:15,911 --> 01:25:19,250
Yes, here we are now in a
mixed forest, and just as Viktor

910
01:25:19,262 --> 01:25:23,010
Schauberger describes, a mixed
forest simply consists of different

911
01:25:23,010 --> 01:25:27,010
Light-demanding tree species
and shade-tolerant tree species.

912
01:25:27,010 --> 01:25:30,810
Here we have a light-demanding tree
species, this is the European beech.

913
01:25:30,810 --> 01:25:32,711
This is the silver fir.

914
01:25:32,711 --> 01:25:35,611
It's clearly a typical shade indicator.

915
01:25:35,611 --> 01:25:37,997
And you can see from
the forest structure that

916
01:25:38,009 --> 01:25:40,510
the woodland is already
built in multiple layers.

917
01:25:40,510 --> 01:25:44,411
This leads to much more
vegetation already being present here.

918
01:25:44,411 --> 01:25:47,211
We basically have more indicator species.

919
01:25:47,211 --> 01:25:51,611
For example, here we have blueberry,
which indicates a rather acidic soil.

920
01:25:51,611 --> 01:25:54,010
We even find a small maple here.

921
01:25:54,211 --> 01:25:57,994
Yes, this means that biodiversity
increases dramatically in this

922
01:25:58,006 --> 01:26:01,510
area, which tells us that the
forest is much more resilient.

923
01:26:01,510 --> 01:26:05,648
That means it can withstand storms
much better, snow pressure has much

924
01:26:05,660 --> 01:26:09,810
less chance. So, this would be the
perfect forest model for the future.

925
01:26:09,810 --> 01:26:14,899
In the present day, however, Matthias
Pointinger still observes environmentally

926
01:26:14,911 --> 01:26:19,310
damaging practices in the forestry
industry in his local environment.

927
01:26:19,310 --> 01:26:22,940
At least on smaller areas,
clear-cuts still occur today,

928
01:26:22,952 --> 01:26:26,211
which Viktor Schauberger
referred to as light cuts.

929
01:26:26,911 --> 01:26:29,211
This one took place about a year ago.

930
01:26:32,510 --> 01:26:34,310
Why do people do this?

931
01:26:34,310 --> 01:26:37,789
To transport the timber
from the forest to the logging

932
01:26:37,801 --> 01:26:40,911
road as cheaply as
possible and maximize profits.

933
01:26:40,911 --> 01:26:43,711
Here, it's clearly all about money again.

934
01:26:44,211 --> 01:26:49,611
And once again, it's being prioritized
over truly nature-appropriate management.

935
01:26:49,611 --> 01:26:51,010
And that needs to change.

936
01:26:51,310 --> 01:26:54,911
Clear-cutting initially
impacts the local ecosystem.

937
01:26:57,810 --> 01:27:01,310
So Viktor Schauberger
viewed this very critically.

938
01:27:01,310 --> 01:27:03,310
And the reason is quite simple.

939
01:27:03,310 --> 01:27:06,911
Because the forest floor is
left completely unprotected.

940
01:27:06,911 --> 01:27:09,510
That means it is very sensitive to erosion.

941
01:27:09,510 --> 01:27:12,153
That means, when heavy
rain comes, the humus and

942
01:27:12,165 --> 01:27:14,711
soil are washed away
and the site is destroyed.

943
01:27:15,111 --> 01:27:19,111
And the intense sunlight
heats up the forest floor.

944
01:27:20,211 --> 01:27:24,260
According to Viktor Schauberger,
this increase in forest floor

945
01:27:24,272 --> 01:27:28,010
temperature has profound
implications for the water cycle.

946
01:27:28,510 --> 01:27:33,510
He speaks of two water cycles:
the complete cycle and the half cycle.

947
01:27:37,540 --> 01:27:42,941
When the forest landscape is still intact,
the forest floor remains relatively cool.

948
01:27:43,141 --> 01:27:48,074
When warmer rainwater meets the cooler
ground, it easily penetrates the soil,

949
01:27:48,086 --> 01:27:52,840
replenishes the groundwater, and
eventually returns to the earth's surface.

950
01:27:52,840 --> 01:27:57,141
It evaporates, forms clouds,
and precipitates again.

951
01:27:58,840 --> 01:28:02,740
Schauberger refers to this
as the complete water cycle.

952
01:28:07,441 --> 01:28:12,141
But this cycle is disrupted by
clear-cutting or deforestation.

953
01:28:13,040 --> 01:28:17,240
Without trees, the ground is
now warmer than the falling rain.

954
01:28:17,240 --> 01:28:21,320
The rainwater barely penetrates
the soil anymore, running

955
01:28:21,332 --> 01:28:25,141
superficially into streams
and rivers, causing floods.

956
01:28:25,141 --> 01:28:28,340
The groundwater level,
on the other hand, drops.

957
01:28:28,941 --> 01:28:32,915
In summer, the surface water
often evaporates right where it

958
01:28:32,927 --> 01:28:37,240
falls, forming additional clouds
and thus generating new rainfall.

959
01:28:37,340 --> 01:28:39,540
One flood causes the next.

960
01:28:40,240 --> 01:28:43,240
Schauberger speaks of the half water cycle.

961
01:28:47,240 --> 01:28:52,040
The cause for the warming of the
soil is, on one hand, the lack of shade.

962
01:28:56,141 --> 01:29:00,540
But a good forester also knows a
second, much more important reason.

963
01:29:00,540 --> 01:29:03,141
In here, I feel very, very good.

964
01:29:03,141 --> 01:29:04,840
That's why I'm still here.

965
01:29:04,840 --> 01:29:07,540
In fact, there's a very cool
breeze blowing in here.

966
01:29:07,540 --> 01:29:09,040
It's hard to believe.

967
01:29:09,040 --> 01:29:12,240
It works—the air here is definitely cool.

968
01:29:12,240 --> 01:29:14,340
And how does the tree accomplish this?

969
01:29:14,340 --> 01:29:16,040
How does it work in the forest?

970
01:29:16,040 --> 01:29:20,740
In the forest, the tree transports
large amounts of water up to its canopy.

971
01:29:20,740 --> 01:29:22,240
There it has its stomata.

972
01:29:22,240 --> 01:29:26,340
There the water evaporates and
there this evaporative cooling occurs.

973
01:29:26,340 --> 01:29:29,718
And this cold, because it is
heavier, settles toward the

974
01:29:29,730 --> 01:29:32,941
ground and then slowly
drifts across the forest floor.

975
01:29:32,941 --> 01:29:34,441
And that’s what we feel here.

976
01:29:34,441 --> 01:29:38,540
So it's a significant contribution
to cooling the entire planet.

977
01:29:39,240 --> 01:29:43,441
Initially, the evaporative
cooling affects the microclimate.

978
01:29:43,441 --> 01:29:49,240
However, Walter Schauberger also recognized
its potential impact on the global climate.

979
01:29:49,240 --> 01:29:54,540
As early as 1962, he warned
about the desertification of Europe.

980
01:29:54,540 --> 01:29:58,340
The term 'climate change'
didn't exist back then.

981
01:29:58,340 --> 01:30:02,109
Schauberger spoke of a man-made
climate aWALAnche, caused

982
01:30:02,121 --> 01:30:05,641
by what he called, quote,
'the skinning of the Earth.'

983
01:30:06,441 --> 01:30:10,240
Without the forest, there
is no evaporative cooling.

984
01:30:11,540 --> 01:30:14,240
Walter Schauberger calculated it out.

985
01:30:14,240 --> 01:30:19,059
A mixed forest in Central
Europe, just one square kilometer

986
01:30:19,071 --> 01:30:23,740
in size, evaporates up to 5
million liters of water daily.

987
01:30:23,740 --> 01:30:29,141
This removes almost 3 billion kilocalories
of heat from the surrounding environment.

988
01:30:29,340 --> 01:30:33,602
According to the comparative
standard at that time,

989
01:30:33,614 --> 01:30:37,641
this equals the heat
content of 500 tons of coal.

990
01:30:40,941 --> 01:30:46,641
Schauberger also calculated the evaporation
effect for the entire water cycle of Earth.

991
01:30:46,740 --> 01:30:53,040
He calculated a heat extraction of
9,600,000,000,000 kilocalories per second.

992
01:30:54,040 --> 01:30:55,740
So the effect is enormous.

993
01:30:56,340 --> 01:31:00,027
It corresponds to removing
the heat content of a

994
01:31:00,039 --> 01:31:04,040
coal mountain weighing
1.6 million tons every second.

995
01:31:06,840 --> 01:31:11,340
This involves what's called latent
heat, which is required for evaporation.

996
01:31:13,240 --> 01:31:16,344
The latent heat transforms
into sensible heat

997
01:31:16,356 --> 01:31:19,540
when evaporation
diminishes or ceases entirely.

998
01:31:22,840 --> 01:31:27,141
In the twenty-first century, nobody
talks about a climate aWALAnche.

999
01:31:27,540 --> 01:31:30,941
But the term climate
change is on everyone's lips.

1000
01:31:31,841 --> 01:31:35,170
There are recurring instances
where scientists question

1001
01:31:35,182 --> 01:31:38,941
whether carbon dioxide emissions
are the sole or primary cause.

1002
01:31:42,040 --> 01:31:46,390
One of them was the late water
ecologist Wilhelm Riepl, who served as

1003
01:31:46,402 --> 01:31:50,640
a professor at the Technical
University of Berlin until his passing.

1004
01:31:51,441 --> 01:31:55,592
In 2013, he distilled Walter
Schauberger's theories on

1005
01:31:55,604 --> 01:31:59,540
evaporative cooling into
their essential principles.

1006
01:32:01,241 --> 01:32:07,927
Quote: The cause of human-induced climate
change is rather the irreversible large-scale

1007
01:32:07,939 --> 01:32:14,941
destruction of landscapes capable of evaporation,
equipped with fertile soil and vegetation.

1008
01:32:19,640 --> 01:32:23,944
Riepl and Schauberger knew
that a multi-layered vegetation cover

1009
01:32:23,956 --> 01:32:28,140
can evaporate more water than
a water surface of the same area.

1010
01:32:29,040 --> 01:32:33,941
Walter Schauberger described
trees as pulsating columns of water.

1011
01:32:34,640 --> 01:32:37,040
And forests as green oceans.

1012
01:32:39,290 --> 01:32:45,290
Forests transport rain across continents
by recharging depleted clouds with water.

1013
01:32:45,890 --> 01:32:48,790
The clouds are, so to speak, saturated.

1014
01:32:49,691 --> 01:32:52,691
Scientists today speak of flying rivers.

1015
01:32:55,691 --> 01:33:00,578
According to Viktor Schauberger,
when trees and vegetation disappear, the

1016
01:33:00,590 --> 01:33:05,290
complete water cycle degrades into
what he termed a 'half water cycle'.

1017
01:33:06,991 --> 01:33:11,459
According to his son Walter
Schauberger, an accompanying effect

1018
01:33:11,471 --> 01:33:16,091
from solar radiation compounds
the decline in evaporative cooling.

1019
01:33:16,091 --> 01:33:19,290
For example, as seen
here at the 20th parallel.

1020
01:33:19,290 --> 01:33:25,524
Where vegetation is absent, energy equivalent
to approximately 1,000 tons of coal per

1021
01:33:25,536 --> 01:33:31,491
square kilometer is wasted daily and
contributes as fuel to the climate aWALAnche.

1022
01:33:35,991 --> 01:33:38,790
Walter Schauberger, 1962.

1023
01:33:43,290 --> 01:33:46,851
This ultimately raises the
question of whether today's

1024
01:33:46,863 --> 01:33:50,890
climate scientists can no
longer see the forest for the trees.

1025
01:33:50,890 --> 01:33:54,655
An interesting line of
thought from my father

1026
01:33:54,667 --> 01:33:58,691
Walter Schauberger,
notably from the early 1960s.

1027
01:34:01,890 --> 01:34:06,991
An approach that could have a stimulating
effect on today's climate research.

1028
01:34:07,491 --> 01:34:12,987
Basically, Walter, just like his father
Viktor Schauberger, is concerned with

1029
01:34:12,999 --> 01:34:18,790
having an intact environment and healthy
forests, and thus preserving clean water.

1030
01:34:22,191 --> 01:34:24,991
Jörg Schauberger on his way to the archive.

1031
01:34:25,091 --> 01:34:29,691
His forefathers left behind
countless pages of written paper.

1032
01:34:30,390 --> 01:34:34,091
Only a small portion of it
has been published so far.

1033
01:34:34,191 --> 01:34:37,191
Even fewer from Walter than from Viktor.

1034
01:34:38,390 --> 01:34:41,950
In our archive, we have
virtually all the documents

1035
01:34:41,962 --> 01:34:45,191
from Walter Schauberger
and Viktor Schauberger.

1036
01:34:45,691 --> 01:34:50,191
Both wrote a great many
letters and a great many essays.

1037
01:34:50,890 --> 01:34:54,426
They corresponded, so to
speak, with everyone under

1038
01:34:54,438 --> 01:34:58,191
the sun, and here we have
tried to organize everything.

1039
01:34:59,491 --> 01:35:03,136
That is, we've worked to
establish a chronological

1040
01:35:03,148 --> 01:35:07,091
sequence of letters from
Viktor and Walter Schauberger.

1041
01:35:09,290 --> 01:35:12,629
In the archive at the
PKS villa, there are also

1042
01:35:12,641 --> 01:35:15,991
over 500 sketches created
by Viktor Schauberger.

1043
01:35:15,991 --> 01:35:20,491
Grandson Jörg is selecting material
for a planned book publication.

1044
01:35:22,790 --> 01:35:26,191
There are countless sketches
by Viktor Schauberger.

1045
01:35:27,691 --> 01:35:32,088
During periods when he had
limited materials for building

1046
01:35:32,100 --> 01:35:36,890
models and prototypes, he
expressed his ideas through drawings.

1047
01:35:37,091 --> 01:35:43,875
Here, for example, is the so-called 'trout
boat.' The concept is that water enters

1048
01:35:43,887 --> 01:35:50,191
at the front and exits more rapidly at
the rear, propelling the boat forward.

1049
01:35:51,691 --> 01:35:55,691
One of the many sketches from
Viktor Schauberger's sketchbook.

1050
01:35:57,691 --> 01:36:01,491
This drawing, we don't really know
what it's supposed to represent.

1051
01:36:01,890 --> 01:36:04,790
But what's interesting
is that it's an envelope.

1052
01:36:06,290 --> 01:36:09,725
So Viktor Schauberger
used anything that had

1053
01:36:09,737 --> 01:36:13,491
space for a sketch, even
a calendar, for example.

1054
01:36:16,591 --> 01:36:21,890
And there he sketches fantastic
devices on both the front and back sides.

1055
01:36:24,991 --> 01:36:29,991
This one, for example, is designed to
be powered by water to drive a dynamo.

1056
01:36:31,691 --> 01:36:34,491
Similarly here with a two-winged whorlpipe.

1057
01:36:37,991 --> 01:36:39,991
Interesting here, for example.

1058
01:36:41,091 --> 01:36:46,591
Another sketch on the same sheet
of a rotor, a propeller, or an impeller.

1059
01:36:47,890 --> 01:36:52,029
And to help us better visualize
it, when he had time, he even

1060
01:36:52,041 --> 01:36:55,991
created quasi three-dimensional
models of such a propeller.

1061
01:36:59,790 --> 01:37:00,991
From that...

1062
01:37:02,890 --> 01:37:06,091
...an actual model was
produced, a finished piece.

1063
01:37:08,091 --> 01:37:10,691
Speaking of models and artifacts.

1064
01:37:10,890 --> 01:37:14,464
Under the hyperbolic
funnel in the PKS villa, Jörg

1065
01:37:14,476 --> 01:37:17,991
Schauberger expresses
a final wish on this matter.

1066
01:37:18,785 --> 01:37:22,373
Viktor and Walter Schauberger
had numerous models and

1067
01:37:22,385 --> 01:37:26,185
prototypes constructed based
on their ideas and sketches.

1068
01:37:26,185 --> 01:37:30,604
Many of these are displayed
here in our showcases, and they are

1069
01:37:30,616 --> 01:37:35,185
waiting to be implemented with
today's technological capabilities.

1070
01:37:59,584 --> 01:38:03,079
I am constantly amazed
at how far my forefathers

1071
01:38:03,091 --> 01:38:06,884
Viktor and Walter Schauberger
looked into the future.

1072
01:38:07,584 --> 01:38:13,184
Equally fascinating are their insights
into the power of water and vortices.

1073
01:38:13,365 --> 01:38:16,166
In this spirit, may the vortex be with you.

